insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 1, 'For 2e-5 per year under `r2p2-workers`, what does `alarpBand` hand back beside its band word?', '["BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE with no demonstration required, because 2e-5 per year sits comfortably below the public upper limit of 1e-4 per year.", "TOLERABLE with `alarpDemonstrationRequired` set true, and the ratios 0.020000 of the upper limit and 20.000000 times the lower limit.", "TOLERABLE with `alarpDemonstrationRequired` set false, since the demonstration is only asked for when a limit is touched exactly.", "UNACCEPTABLE, and the ratios 20.000000 of the upper limit and 0.020000 times the lower limit, read the other way round."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Between 1e-6 and 1e-3 per year a worker individual risk is TOLERABLE only if reduced as low as reasonably practicable, so the engine sets `alarpDemonstrationRequired` true, and it returns 0.020000 of the upper limit and 20.000000 times the lower. The public upper limit plays no part against the worker preset, the demonstration flag is set across the whole middle region, and the swapped ratios invert both quantities.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 2, 'What thresholds do the two R2P2 presets `r2p2-workers` and `r2p2-public` carry in the engine?', '["Workers unacceptable above 1e-4 per year and the public above 1e-3, with both broadly acceptable at or below 1e-6 per year.", "Workers unacceptable above 1e-3 per year and broadly acceptable at or below 1e-5, the public above 1e-4 and at or below 1e-6.", "Both unacceptable above 1e-3 per year, with the public preset differing only in its lower limit of 1e-7 per year.", "Workers unacceptable above 1e-3 per year and the public above 1e-4, with both broadly acceptable at or below 1e-6 per year."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course shows the presets: workers above 1e-3 and at or below 1e-6, the public above 1e-4 and at or below 1e-6. Swapping the two upper limits puts the looser limit on the public. There is no 1e-5 lower limit for workers and no 1e-7 lower limit for the public, so the other two pairs are invented thresholds.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 3, 'An individual risk of 5e-4 per year is banded under each preset in turn. Which pair of band words comes back?', '["TOLERABLE against `r2p2-workers` and UNACCEPTABLE against `r2p2-public`, because it lies between 1e-4 and 1e-3.", "UNACCEPTABLE against both presets, since any individual risk above 1e-4 per year is intolerable whoever carries it.", "TOLERABLE against both presets, since 5e-4 per year lies below the upper limit of 1e-3 that R2P2 prints for everyone.", "TOLERABLE against `r2p2-public` and UNACCEPTABLE against `r2p2-workers`, the looser limit belonging to the public."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The worker upper limit is 1e-3 per year and the public one is 1e-4, so 5e-4 is TOLERABLE for a worker and UNACCEPTABLE for a member of the public, which is the row the course shows. The upper limit of 1e-4 applies to the public preset only, 1e-3 applies to workers only, and the reversed pair gives the public the looser limit.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 4, 'A worker''s individual risk sits exactly on the upper limit, 1e-3 per year. Which band holds it, and on whose authority?', '["UNACCEPTABLE, because R2P2''s bias to safety puts every value sitting at a threshold in the worse band, and the engine follows that reading.", "TOLERABLE with no boundary named, because the engine rounds 1e-3 down to the band below before it compares anything at all.", "TOLERABLE with the boundary `unacceptable` named, by the owner''s decision that a value exactly at a threshold belongs to the lower band.", "A refusal, because a value exactly on a threshold is ambiguous and the engine asks the caller to move it off the line."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course records it as the owner''s decision: a value exactly at a threshold belongs to the lower band, so 1e-3 is TOLERABLE for workers and the result names the boundary `unacceptable` it touched. R2P2''s bias to safety is the argument the other way, which the engine declined. Nothing is rounded, and the boundary is reported, never refused.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 5, 'A member of the public carries exactly 1e-6 per year. Where does the engine place that value?', '["TOLERABLE, with the boundary `broadly-acceptable` named and an ALARP demonstration required because the value is on the limit.", "BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, with the boundary `broadly-acceptable` named and no ALARP demonstration required.", "BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, with no boundary named, because a lower limit is written at or below and so is never touched by the engine.", "TOLERABLE, with no boundary named, because the lower limit is exclusive and 1e-6 per year has to fall strictly below it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The lower limit is written ''at or below'', so 1e-6 per year is BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, the flag is false, and the engine still names the boundary `broadly-acceptable` that the value sits on, as the course''s row shows. Putting the value in TOLERABLE would place a threshold in the upper band, which is the convention the engine did not take, and the boundary name is reported at either limit.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 6, 'Three stated factors, 0.1 x 0.1 x 0.1, multiply to 0.0010000000000000002 in IEEE double. Against the worker preset, what band does the engine return, and why?', '["UNACCEPTABLE, because the product lies above 1e-3 in double and the engine compares the value exactly as it arrives.", "UNACCEPTABLE with the boundary `unacceptable` named, because the engine flags the touch and then bands the value above it.", "A refusal, because the engine detects the rounding error in the product.", "TOLERABLE with the boundary `unacceptable`, because a value within 1e-9 relative of a threshold IS the threshold."]'::jsonb, 3, 'BOUNDARY_SNAP is 1e-9 relative: a computed value that close to a threshold is taken as the threshold, so the product stays TOLERABLE for workers and `atBoundary` names `unacceptable`. Without the snap a plain comparison would call it UNACCEPTABLE, although the exact product is the threshold itself. The engine never refuses a value for being close to a limit.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 7, 'An individual risk of 1.0000001e-6 per year, one part in ten million above the lower limit, is banded against the public preset. What comes back?', '["BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE with the boundary `broadly-acceptable`, because the snap treats anything within a part in ten million as the limit.", "BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE with the boundary null, because the engine rounds every individual risk to six significant figures before banding.", "TOLERABLE with the boundary null, because the snap is 1e-9 relative and this value lies outside it.", "TOLERABLE with the boundary `broadly-acceptable`, because the engine names the nearest limit whenever the value is close to one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The snap is narrow: 1e-9 relative. One part in ten million is a hundred times wider, so 1.0000001e-6 per year is TOLERABLE for the public with boundary null, the golden case the course shows. Reading the snap as a part in ten million widens it wrongly, the engine rounds nothing before banding, and a boundary is named only when the value is taken as the threshold.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 8, 'Which sources does the course cite for the claim that every source the engine read words its threshold so that equality falls in the lower band?', '["R2P2 paragraph 136 says \"more than\" and Bevi says \"ten hoogste\" (at most), and the checklist says costs over benefits \"greater than\" the DF.", "The Purple Book Figure 6.8 caption, \"F < 1e-3 N^-2\", and R2P2''s bias to safety, which together put a value lying on a line in the band above it.", "R2P2 paragraph 136 says \"at least\" and Bevi says \"more than\", and the engine therefore rounds a value at the threshold to the band below.", "The checklist says costs over benefits \"at least\" the DF and R2P2 says limits are guidelines, so equality is left to the analyst."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course quotes R2P2 paragraph 136 "more than", Bevi "ten hoogste" and the checklist "greater than", each of which puts equality in the lower band. The Figure 6.8 caption and R2P2''s bias to safety are the two things that argue the other way, so they cannot support the claim. Neither "at least" wording appears in the course, and the engine rounds nothing.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 9, 'Which argument does the course name as pointing the OTHER way from the owner''s decision on a value exactly at a threshold?', '["The IEEE double product 0.0010000000000000002, which shows the exact product lying above 1e-3 per year and so in the upper band at every threshold.", "Bevi''s wording \"ten hoogste\", which the course translates as at least and which puts a value on the Dutch line in the band above.", "R2P2''s bias to safety, which could argue that a value at the limit should fall in the worse band, together with the Figure 6.8 caption that reads the other way at equality.", "The checklist''s DF of more than 10 being unlikely, which argues that a cost at DF times the benefit is always grossly disproportionate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course names R2P2''s bias to safety as the argument for the other convention, and notes the Purple Book Figure 6.8 caption, "F < 1e-3 N^-2", reads the other way at equality. The double product is a rounding artefact that the snap exists to absorb. "Ten hoogste" means at most, which supports the lower band, and the DF note says nothing about equality.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 10, 'The R2P2 box prints the 1999/00 service sector rate as 1 in 388 565 a year, which the golden writes as 0.000002573572 per year. What band does the engine return against the worker preset?', '["BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, because R2P2 says worker rates are normally well below the upper limit of 1 in 1000 a year.", "TOLERABLE, because it is still above 1 in a million, although well below the upper limit.", "BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, because the box bands every industry rate against the public preset whatever its source names.", "UNACCEPTABLE, because a rate read from a published statistic counts every death across the whole workforce at once."]'::jsonb, 1, '0.000002573572 per year sits above the worker lower limit of 1e-6 and below the upper limit of 1e-3, so the engine returns TOLERABLE, and the box''s own note says it is still above 1 in a million. Being well below the upper limit is not the same as being broadly acceptable. Each rate is banded against the preset its source names, which for the service sector is workers.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 11, 'The R2P2 box''s public gas rate, 1 in 1 510 000 over 1994/5 to 1998/9, is written 0.000000662252 per year. Against which preset is it banded, and what comes back?', '["Against `r2p2-workers`, and TOLERABLE, because a rate read from an industry statistic always carries a worker''s ALARP demonstration along with it.", "Against `r2p2-public`, and TOLERABLE with the boundary `broadly-acceptable`, because the value lies inside the snap of 1e-6 per year.", "Against `r2p2-public`, and UNACCEPTABLE, because every rate carried by a member of the public is judged against the upper limit of 1e-4 per year.", "Against `r2p2-public`, and BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, matching the source''s words \"below the limit of what is often regarded as broadly acceptable\"."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course bands each rate against the preset its source names, and the public gas rate goes against `r2p2-public`: 0.000000662252 per year is below 1e-6, so it is BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, as the source says. It is nowhere near the 1e-9 relative snap of the lower limit, it lies far below the public upper limit of 1e-4, and a worker preset would not be the preset its source names.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 12, '`alarpBand` is called with an individual risk and no thresholds at all. What does the engine return?', '["A refusal naming `thresholds`, in the engine''s own words: thresholds: a preset name or { unacceptableAbovePerYr, broadlyAcceptableAtOrBelowPerYr } is required", "The band against `r2p2-workers`, the preset the engine falls back on whenever the caller has not named another one.", "The band against `r2p2-public`, the stricter preset, which the engine chooses when nothing is given because it errs to safety.", "The two ratios with a band of null, so that the analyst can read the individual risk against limits of their own later."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The quoted message is the engine''s own, and it names the field `thresholds`. The engine invents no input, so there is no default preset, strict or loose, and a refusal returns an error in place of any result, which rules out ratios with a null band.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 13, 'A caller passes the thresholds preset name `constructor`, a name every JavaScript object carries. What does the engine do?', '["It resolves the name to the object constructor, finds no limits there and bands every individual risk as BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE.", "It ignores the unknown name and bands the individual risk against `r2p2-workers`, the first preset in its own list of presets.", "It throws an uncaught type error, since the name resolves to a function where the engine expects an object of limits.", "It refuses, in its own words: thresholds: unknown preset ''constructor''; one of r2p2-workers, r2p2-public, or give { unacceptableAbovePerYr, broadlyAcceptableAtOrBelowPerYr }"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A preset name is looked up only among the names the engine itself defines, so `constructor` is refused like any other unknown name, and the message quoted is the engine''s own. It never resolves an inherited name, never falls back to a preset it was not given, and returns a refusal object with a field rather than throwing.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 14, 'A caller gives thresholds of their own with `unacceptableAbovePerYr` of 1e-6 and `broadlyAcceptableAtOrBelowPerYr` of 1e-3. What does the engine return?', '["A band computed after the engine quietly swaps the two limits into the order it expects, with a note added in the basis block to say that it did so.", "A refusal naming the lower limit, in the engine''s own words: thresholds.broadlyAcceptableAtOrBelowPerYr: must lie below unacceptableAbovePerYr", "UNACCEPTABLE for every individual risk above 1e-6 per year, since the upper limit is taken exactly as the caller typed it.", "BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE for every individual risk at or below 1e-3 per year, since the lower limit is applied before the upper one."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Thresholds in the wrong order are refused, and the quoted message is the engine''s own, naming `thresholds.broadlyAcceptableAtOrBelowPerYr`. The engine does not repair a caller''s input by swapping it, and it does not band anything against an inverted pair, which is why the two partial readings are both wrong.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-the-alarp-regions', 15, 'The two exported constants BOUNDARY_SNAP and BRANCH_SUM_TOLERANCE are both 1e-9. How do they differ in the way the engine applies them?', '["BOUNDARY_SNAP is absolute on an individual risk per year, while BRANCH_SUM_TOLERANCE is relative to the size of the tree.", "They are one constant exported under two names, and the engine applies the same absolute test wherever a value meets a limit.", "BOUNDARY_SNAP is relative to the threshold it tests, while BRANCH_SUM_TOLERANCE is absolute on the sum of a branch set.", "BOUNDARY_SNAP rounds an individual risk to nine decimals before banding, and BRANCH_SUM_TOLERANCE rounds each branch."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course describes BOUNDARY_SNAP as 1e-9 relative, a computed value within that distance of a threshold being the threshold, and BRANCH_SUM_TOLERANCE as 1e-9 absolute on a branch sum. An absolute 1e-9 would swamp an individual risk near 1e-6 per year, so the snap cannot be absolute. Neither constant rounds anything; each decides when two values count as equal.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 1, 'How does the engine build the yearly benefit of a measure that lowers the PLL by deltaPLL fatalities a year?', '["deltaPLL times the VPF alone, since injuries prevented are a matter for the ALARP note and never enter the arithmetic of the benefit.", "The VPF divided by deltaPLL, which gives the value of each fatality the measure prevents over a year of its operation.", "deltaPLL times the VPF, plus the sum over the injury and ill health lines of expected cases a year times the value per case.", "deltaPLL times the VPF times the DF, so that the gross disproportion factor is already carried inside the yearly benefit."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course writes B = deltaPLL x VPF + the sum of expected cases a year times the value per case, and the checklist example carries three injury lines through exactly that sum. Leaving the injuries out drops real benefit. Dividing the VPF by deltaPLL inverts the product, and the DF belongs to the test that compares cost with benefit, never to the benefit itself.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 2, 'The EDIKAN firewall lowers the PLL by 2e-3 a year with a VPF of 1000000 over a life of 20 years. Undiscounted, what are its fatality benefit a year and the present value of the benefit?', '["2000.00 a year, and 40000.00 over the life.", "2000.00 a year, and 34337.28 over the life, at the checklist limits.", "40000.00 a year, and 350000.00 over the life.", "2000.00 a year, and 2000.00 over the life."]'::jsonb, 0, '2e-3 times 1000000 is 2000.00 a year, and twenty year-end flows of it at no discount give 40000.00, as the course shows. 34337.28 is the same benefit at a benefit rate of 0.015, a different convention from the undiscounted one asked for. 40000.00 is the life''s benefit and 350000.00 the cost, and 2000.00 over the life forgets nineteen of the twenty years.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 3, '`costBenefit` is called for the EDIKAN firewall with every input but the VPF. What does the engine return?', '["A result computed at the 2003 checklist figure of 1336800, the most recent HSE value the engine exports, with a note in its basis block.", "A result computed at the R2P2 figure of 1000000, which is the engine''s default VPF whenever the caller leaves the field out of a call.", "A result with a benefit of zero, so the verdict comes back GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE for any measure that has a cost of its own.", "A refusal naming `vpf`, in the engine''s own words: vpf: the value of preventing a fatality is required, above 0; HSE figures are illustrative only (HSE_ILLUSTRATIVE_VALUES)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'VPF has no default, and a call without one is refused with the message quoted, which is the engine''s own. Both HSE figures are exported for illustration only, so the engine uses neither of them in place of a missing input. A benefit of zero would be the engine inventing a number, which it never does.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 4, 'What status does the engine give the two HSE values it exports as HSE_ILLUSTRATIVE_VALUES, 1000000 and 1336800?', '["Defaults, the older applied when the caller names the 2001 convention and the newer applied whenever a call arrives with no VPF at all, as the basis block of the result then records.", "Illustration only, each in GBP at its own price year, and neither a recommendation, because current HSE figures were not found on a live page.", "Bounds, so that the engine refuses any VPF the caller gives that lies below 1000000 or above 1336800 at the prices of today.", "Current recommendations, 1336800 being the value HSE publishes now and 1000000 kept only for the reproduction of older work."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course is plain: both figures are exported for illustration only, in GBP at their price year, a call without a VPF is refused, and current HSE figures were not found on a live page, so neither is a recommendation. They are not defaults, the engine bounds a VPF only by requiring it above 0, and the 2003 value is a dated figure.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 5, 'R2P2 Appendix 3 paragraph 13 says that with a VPF of 1000000 a reduction of one in a hundred thousand in one person''s individual risk is worth about 10. What does the engine give for deltaPLL 1e-5 over 1 year?', '["A benefit of 10.00 a year for each of 20 years, because the engine applies the EDIKAN life whenever a caller asks for one year.", "A benefit of 40000.00, because the paragraph''s figure is carried at the full EDIKAN life of 20 years before it is printed.", "A benefit of 10.00, which reproduces the published figure.", "No benefit at all, because a single year of life is refused by the engine as too short a period to weigh against a cost."]'::jsonb, 2, '1e-5 times 1000000 is 10.00 for one year, which is the golden case the course shows and the published margin note reproduced. The engine takes the life the caller states, so the EDIKAN life of 20 years has no part in this call, and 40000.00 is the EDIKAN benefit. A lifetime of 1 year is a whole number of years, 1 or more, which the engine accepts.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 6, 'In the HSE CBA checklist example the fatality line is deltaPLL 0.0002 at a value of 1336800 a case over 25 years, undiscounted. What does the engine give for that line?', '["2072.00, the permanent incapacity line", "9283.50, the whole benefit", "512.50", "6684.00"]'::jsonb, 3, '0.0002 x 1336800 x 25 is 6684.00, and the checklist prints 6684. 2072.00 is the permanent incapacity line, 512.50 the serious injury line, and 9283.50 the total of all four lines, so each is a real figure from the example read against the wrong question.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 7, 'The checklist''s permanently incapacitating injury line carries 0.0004 cases a year at 207200 a case over 25 years. What benefit does that line add over the life?', '["2072.00, which is also what the checklist prints.", "15.00, the slight injury line.", "6684.00, the fatality line, which the ICAF counts.", "9283.50, the whole benefit of the example."]'::jsonb, 0, '0.0004 x 207200 x 25 is 2072.00, and the checklist prints 2072. 15.00 and 6684.00 are the slight injury and fatality lines, and 9283.50 is the total of all four lines. A benefit line is cases a year times value per case times years, undiscounted here.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 8, 'The checklist''s serious injury line comes out at 512.50 in the engine, and the checklist prints 512. What does the difference show?', '["That the engine applies a small discount to injury lines which the undiscounted checklist leaves out, so the two differ by half a unit.", "That the source rounded its printed figure, a property of the SOURCE, while the engine carries the exact product of the stated inputs.", "That the engine mistranscribed the serious injury value per case, which the checklist prints as 20500 and the golden reads differently.", "That the checklist counts whole cases only, so a fraction of a serious injury in a year is dropped before the value is applied."]'::jsonb, 1, '0.001 x 20500 x 25 is 512.50 exactly, and the checklist prints 512: the difference is the source''s rounding, which the course describes as a property of the source. The example is undiscounted in both, the golden uses 20500 as printed, and expected cases a year need not be whole, as the stated 0.001 shows.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 9, 'What total benefit does the engine give for the whole checklist example, and what does the checklist print?', '["9283 in both, because the engine rounds every money figure to a whole unit before it adds the lines, as the checklist does.", "92835.00 in the engine, printed 93000 in the checklist, because the total is carried at the DF of 10 the example uses throughout.", "9283.50 in the engine, printed 9283 in the checklist, the half coming from the serious injury line.", "6684.00 in the engine, printed 6684 in the checklist, because the fatality line is the whole of the benefit a checklist counts."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The four lines, 6684.00, 2072.00, 512.50 and 15.00, sum to 9283.50, and the checklist prints 9283. The engine rounds nothing before summing. 92835.00 and 93000 are the largest reasonable cost at DF 10, computed and printed, which is a different quantity from the benefit. The fatality line alone ignores the three injury lines.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 10, 'The checklist prints the largest reasonable cost of its measure at DF 10 as 93000. What does the engine compute from the stated inputs?', '["93000, because the engine multiplies the printed total 9283 by 10 and rounds the answer up to the nearest thousand as the source does.", "9283.50, because the engine reports the benefit and leaves the multiplication by the DF to the analyst who reads the result.", "The fatality line of 6684.00 times the DF of 10, since the engine''s limit counts no injury line at all.", "92835.00, which is DF 10 times the total benefit of 9283.50."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine returns DF times the present value of the benefit, 10 x 9283.50 = 92835.00, from the inputs. The checklist''s 93000 is a rounded print, 9283.50 is the benefit alone, a product built on the printed total would inherit the source''s rounding, which the engine never reads, and the fatality line alone drops the injury lines the benefit includes.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 11, 'A cost of exactly the printed 93000 is weighed against the checklist measure at DF 10. What verdict does the engine return, and what does it teach?', '["GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, by 165.00, an artefact of the source''s rounding, so an ALARP note computes from the inputs and never from a rounded figure.", "NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because 93000 is the checklist''s own printed limit and a cost at the limit belongs to the lower band.", "NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE with `atBoundary` true, because the snap of 1e-9 relative absorbs a difference as small as 165.00.", "GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, by the half unit that separates the printed total benefit of 9283 from the computed 9283.50."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The computed limit is 92835.00, so 93000 lies above it by 165.00 and the engine returns GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE. The checklist meant "in the region of", and the verdict is an artefact of its rounding. The lower band applies at the computed limit, the snap is far narrower than 165.00, and the half unit on the benefit is only a small part of 165.00 even at a DF of 10: most of the margin is the print''s rounding up to 93000.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 12, 'The checklist table prints the permanent incapacity value mistyped with an extra digit. Which figure does the course use, and why?', '["The mistyped table value, because a published table outranks the arithmetic of a worked example that sits under it.", "207200, because the checklist''s own worked example uses 207,200 and the golden does too.", "Both, running the example twice and quoting whichever verdict is the more favourable to adopting the measure being weighed.", "Neither, since a value printed two ways is withheld and the permanent incapacity line is dropped from the benefit."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course notes the table''s mistyped value and says the checklist''s worked example uses 207200, as the golden does, so that is the figure the course carries, and it reproduces the printed 2072. Choosing the more favourable verdict is not a method, and dropping the line throws away a benefit whose value the example itself confirms.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 13, 'A caller describes a measure that RAISES the PLL by passing a negative deltaPLL. What does the engine return?', '["A negative benefit, so the cost to benefit ratio comes back negative and the verdict is read as GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE.", "A benefit computed on the magnitude of deltaPLL, since the engine takes the absolute value of any reduction it is handed.", "A refusal, in its own words: deltaPllPerYr: must be the reduction in PLL, 0 or more fatalities per year (a measure that raises risk has no benefit to weigh)", "A verdict of NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because a measure with no benefit carries no disproportion to weigh at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine refuses a negative deltaPLL with the message quoted, which is its own. It never computes a negative benefit or ratio, it never silently flips a sign, and a verdict needs a benefit to weigh, which a measure that raises the PLL does not have.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 14, 'The two HSE VPF figures carry price years. What does the engine''s export say about each?', '["1000000 is the 2003 Q3 checklist value and 1336800 the R2P2 figure uprated to 2001 prices by the benefit growth of 4 percent a year.", "Both are 2003 figures, one for workers and one for the public, matching the two tolerability presets the engine carries.", "Both are current figures, with 1336800 applying to fatalities from fire and 1000000 to fatalities from a toxic release.", "1000000 is about GBP 1 000 000 at 2001 prices from R2P2, and 1336800 is the 2003 Q3 checklist fatality value, times 2 for cancer."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The export''s sources are verbatim in the course: R2P2 (2001) Appendix 3 para 13, about GBP 1 000 000 at 2001 prices, and the CBA checklist (2003 values), fatality GBP 1,336,800, times 2 for cancer. The years are not swapped, the two figures do not map to the worker and public presets, and neither is current.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-the-benefit-of-a-measure', 15, 'The checklist example is worked undiscounted over 25 years. Which call through `costBenefit` reproduces its benefit?', '["deltaPLL 0.0002, a VPF of 1336800, the three injury lines as other harms, a life of 25 years and every rate left at its default of zero.", "deltaPLL 0.0002, a VPF of 1336800 and a life of 25 years, with the injury lines left out because the engine counts fatalities alone.", "deltaPLL 0.0002, a VPF of 1000000 and a life of 25 years, since the R2P2 figure is the one the engine applies to the checklist at all times.", "deltaPLL 0.0002, a VPF of 1336800 and a life of 25 years, with the checklist limits of 0.015 on benefits and 0.035 on costs."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every rate defaults to zero, which is undiscounted as in the checklist example, and the injury lines enter as other harms, which is how the engine reaches 9283.50. Leaving the injuries out gives the fatality line alone, 6684.00. The checklist values its fatality at 1336800, and the 2003 rate limits belong to a discounted convention the example does not use.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 1, 'When the engine puts the EDIKAN firewall''s cost and benefit on one footing for the gross disproportion test, when does each flow fall?', '["Capital at the end of year 1, and each annual cost and benefit at the middle of its year, which is the convention the checklist prints for its example.", "Capital at year 0, and each annual cost and each year''s benefit at the END of years 1 to n, through the economics engine''s canonical year-end present value.", "Every flow at the start of its year, capital included, so the first year''s benefit is counted in full before any discount at all is applied to it, which is the convention R2P2 prints.", "Capital spread evenly over the life, and each benefit at the end of its year, so that the cost and the benefit fall in step."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course says every present value goes through the canonical year-end present value: capital at year 0, each annual cost and benefit at the end of years 1 to n. The checklist example is undiscounted, so it prints no timing convention of its own. Start of year and mid-year flows are other conventions, and the engine never spreads capital over the life.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 2, 'The discount and growth rates of a `costBenefit` call are all left out. What does the engine do with them?', '["It refuses the call, because the two HSE sources disagree on the rates and the engine will not choose between them for the caller.", "It applies the 2003 checklist limits, 0.015 on benefits and 0.035 on costs, since they are the more recent of the two HSE sets.", "It applies R2P2''s 6 percent real on both sides with benefits uprated by 4 percent a year, the older and longer standing set.", "Each rate defaults to zero, which is undiscounted, as in the checklist example."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every rate defaults to zero, and zero is undiscounted, which is how the checklist example reproduces. A rate is an input like any other, so the engine never picks a convention for the caller, and a missing rate is no refusal: a rate of zero is a stated, defensible convention in its own right.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 3, 'Which two HSE discounting conventions does the course set side by side?', '["R2P2 (2001), 6 percent real with benefits uprated by 4 percent a year, and the CBA checklist (2003), benefits at no more than 1.5 percent and costs at no less than 3.5 percent.", "R2P2 (2001), benefits at no more than 1.5 percent and costs at no less than 3.5 percent, and the CBA checklist (2003), 6 percent real with a 4 percent uprating.", "R2P2 (2001), 3.5 percent on both sides, and the CBA checklist (2003), 6 percent on costs with no discount at all on benefits, since benefits are lives.", "R2P2 (2001) and the CBA checklist (2003) both at 6 percent real, differing only in whether benefits are uprated by 4 percent or held flat over the life."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course says the two sources disagree: R2P2 discounts at 6 percent real and uprates benefits by 4 percent a year, and the checklist discounts benefits at no more than 1.5 percent and costs at no less than 3.5 percent. Giving R2P2 the 1.5 and 3.5 percent limits swaps the two sources, and the remaining pairs describe conventions neither source states.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 4, 'The EDIKAN firewall (deltaPLL 2e-3, VPF 1000000, 20 years, capital 250000 and 5000 a year) is weighed at the 2003 checklist limits. What present values and ratio does the engine return?', '["A benefit of 40000.00, a cost of 350000.00 and a cost to benefit ratio of 8.750000, which is the undiscounted row.", "A benefit of 32946.93, a cost of 307349.61 and a cost to benefit ratio of 9.328625, which is the R2P2 row.", "A benefit of 34337.28, a cost of 321062.02 and a cost to benefit ratio of 9.350247.", "A benefit of 34337.28 and a cost of 350000.00, the benefit alone discounted."]'::jsonb, 2, 'At benefits 0.015 and costs 0.035 the engine returns 34337.28, 321062.02 and 9.350247. 40000.00, 350000.00 and 8.750000 are the undiscounted row, and 32946.93, 307349.61 and 9.328625 the R2P2 row, each the right arithmetic under a different convention. Discounting the benefit alone leaves the cost undiscounted, a mixed convention that neither HSE source describes.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 5, 'Under R2P2 Appendix 3''s convention, 0.06 on both sides with benefit growth of 0.04, what cost to benefit ratio does the engine return for the EDIKAN firewall?', '["9.350247, from present values of 34337.28 and 321062.02, the 2003 checklist limits.", "8.750000, from present values of 40000.00 and 350000.00, the undiscounted row.", "A ratio built on 40000.00 and 307349.61, the growth of 0.04 cancelling the discount of 0.06 on the benefit side.", "9.328625, from present values of 32946.93 and 307349.61."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The R2P2 row of the course''s table shows 32946.93, 307349.61 and 9.328625. 9.350247 belongs to the checklist limits and 8.750000 to the undiscounted row. Growth of 0.04 against a discount of 0.06 does not cancel, so the benefit is not left at its undiscounted 40000.00.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 6, 'The engine''s ICAF for the EDIKAN firewall at the 2003 checklist limits is 8026550.41. How is it built?', '["The present value of the cost, 321062.02, over the discounted fatalities, 0.034337, the present value of the benefit over the VPF, which is 9350246.71.", "The present value of the cost, 321062.02, over the fatalities prevented, 0.040000, which is deltaPLL times the life in years, UNDISCOUNTED.", "The undiscounted cost, 350000.00, over the fatalities prevented, 0.040000, the undiscounted ICAF.", "The present value of the benefit, 34337.28, over the fatalities prevented, 0.040000."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ICAF is the present value of the cost over deltaPLL times the life in years, undiscounted: 321062.02 over 0.040000 is 8026550.41. Dividing by the discounted fatalities gives 9350246.71, a number the engine never returns. 350000.00 over 0.040000 is the undiscounted row''s 8750000.00, and the present value of the benefit over the fatalities prevented divides a benefit where the ICAF divides a cost.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 7, 'What does the engine return as the ICAF of the EDIKAN firewall with every rate at zero?', '["8750000.00", "8026550.41, the checklist row", "7683740.15, the R2P2 row", "9350246.71"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Undiscounted, the cost is 350000.00 and the fatalities prevented 0.040000, so the ICAF is 8750000.00. 8026550.41 and 7683740.15 are the ICAFs at the 2003 checklist limits and under R2P2''s convention. 9350246.71 divides the checklist cost by discounted fatalities, a number the engine never returns at any convention.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 8, 'Across the three conventions the EDIKAN firewall''s ICAF moves from 8750000.00 to 8026550.41 to 7683740.15. What stays fixed, and at what figure?', '["The present value of the cost, 350000.00, because only the benefit is discounted when an ICAF is formed from a measure.", "The ratio of cost to benefit, 8.750000, because an ICAF is a ratio and discounting both sides of a ratio leaves it unmoved.", "The fatalities prevented, 0.040000, because only the cost is discounted.", "The discounted fatalities, 0.034337, because the engine discounts the fatalities once at the checklist limits and holds them."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course says the fatalities prevented stay at 0.040000 whatever the rate, and only the cost is discounted, which is why the ICAF falls as the cost rate rises. The cost moves from 350000.00 to 321062.02 to 307349.61. The ratio moves too, from 8.750000 to 9.350247 and 9.328625, and 0.034337 is a derived figure the engine never uses as its denominator.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 9, 'What is the ICAF of the EDIKAN firewall under R2P2 Appendix 3''s convention?', '["8026550.41, the present value of the cost at the checklist limits over the same 0.040000 fatalities prevented by the firewall.", "8750000.00, since R2P2''s benefit growth and discount rate cancel and leave the undiscounted ICAF of the firewall in place.", "9350246.71, the checklist cost over the discounted fatalities.", "7683740.15, the present value of the cost, 307349.61, over 0.040000 fatalities prevented."]'::jsonb, 3, 'R2P2''s 6 percent on costs brings the cost down to 307349.61, the lowest of the three, so its ICAF is the lowest too, 7683740.15, with the same 0.040000 in the denominator. The two larger figures belong to the checklist and zero rate conventions. 9350246.71 divides the checklist cost by discounted fatalities, a number the engine never returns.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 10, 'What is the ICAF, in the engine''s vocabulary and in R2P2''s?', '["The implied cost of averting a fatality, which R2P2 calls the VPF, since both are a sum of money set against one death.", "The implied cost of averting a fatality, which R2P2 Appendix 3 paragraph 15 calls the cost per fatality prevented, CPF.", "The individual cost of a fatal accident, which R2P2 calls the FAR, per 100,000,000 exposed hours.", "The incremental cost of avoiding a fire, which R2P2 calls the CPF."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course defines the ICAF as the engine''s cost per fatality prevented, CPF in R2P2 Appendix 3 paragraph 15: the present value of the cost over the undiscounted fatalities prevented. The VPF is a value set by the analyst, a different quantity. FAR is the fatal accident rate, and the acronym does not stand for a cost of avoiding fires.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 11, 'When can the EDIKAN firewall''s ICAF be read straight against the VPF to decide the gross disproportion test, and when must the analyst read the cost to benefit ratio against the DF instead?', '["When injuries add nothing, an ICAF below VPF times the DF passes; with injuries in the benefit the ratio is compared with the DF, because the ICAF alone leaves them out.", "Always, because the ICAF already counts every injury and ill health line of the benefit through the VPF that the analyst sets.", "Never, because the ICAF is formed at a discount while the VPF is undiscounted, so the two cannot be compared on any footing at all.", "Only when every rate is zero, because at any other convention the ICAF and the verdict are guaranteed to disagree about the measure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course says an ICAF below VPF times the DF passes when injuries add nothing, and with injuries in the benefit the ratio against the DF is the test, since the ICAF leaves them out. The ICAF counts fatalities only. It can be read against the VPF at any convention, and nothing guarantees a disagreement away from zero rates.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 12, 'The golden R2P2 case with 6 percent on both sides and a 4 percent uprating of benefits runs through the engine. What present values does it return?', '["A benefit of 32946.93 and a cost of 307349.61, the EDIKAN firewall under the same R2P2 rates.", "A benefit of 9283.50 and a cost of 92835.00, the undiscounted checklist example.", "A benefit of 22635.17 and a cost of 214412.08.", "A cost of 22635.17, a benefit of 214412.08."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course shows the golden R2P2 case through the engine as a benefit of 22635.17 and a cost of 214412.08. 32946.93 and 307349.61 are the EDIKAN firewall under the same convention, a different measure. 9283.50 and 92835.00 are the checklist benefit and its largest reasonable cost, and the reversed pair swaps the two present values.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 13, 'A `costBenefit` call gives the firewall a life of twenty and a half years. What does the engine return, in its own words?', '["A present value over twenty one years, the part year rounded up so that the last half year of benefit still counts.", "A present value over 20 years, the half year dropped because a year-end flow cannot fall in the middle of a year.", "A present value over the full span, the last flow prorated to half its size and placed at the end of the half year.", "lifetimeYears: must be a whole number of years, 1 or more (flows are year-end)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The quoted message is the engine''s own refusal of a lifetime that is not whole years, and it gives the reason: flows are year-end. A refusal returns no present value, so the engine neither rounds the life up or down nor prorates a last flow.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 14, 'At the checklist limits, which discounting string does the firewall result carry, word for word?', '["engines/economics/cashflow.ts npv (mid-year discounting); benefits at 0.035 growing at 0, costs at 0.015; discounted", "engines/economics/cashflow.ts npv (year-end discounting); benefits at 0.015 growing at 0, costs at 0.035; discounted", "engines/economics/cashflow.ts npv (year-end discounting); benefits at 0.06 growing at 0.04, costs at 0.06; discounted", "engines/economics/cashflow.ts npv (year-end discounting); benefits at 0 growing at 0, costs at 0; undiscounted"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course quotes the checklist row''s basis verbatim, with benefits at 0.015 growing at 0 and costs at 0.035. The mid-year string swaps the rates and names a convention the engine does not use. The 0.06 string is the R2P2 row''s and the zero string the undiscounted row''s, each a real string for a different convention.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-discounting-and-the-icaf', 15, 'Why does this course use a present value at all, when present value mechanics belong to the economics courses?', '["Only to put a cost and a benefit on the same footing for the gross disproportion test and the ICAF, through the economics engine''s canonical year-end present value.", "To rank measures by their financial return before the gross disproportion test, so that the cheapest measure is always weighed first.", "To replace the VPF with a market valuation of a life, which the economics engine derives from the discount rate the caller supplies.", "To discount the fatalities prevented so that a death in year 20 of the life counts for less than one prevented in the first year."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course says it uses present value mechanics only to put a cost and a benefit on the same footing for the gross disproportion test, and the ICAF uses the present value of the cost. No financial ranking is made, the VPF stays a stated input, and the fatalities prevented are counted undiscounted.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 1, 'In the HSE checklist''s test, when is a measure NOT reasonably practicable?', '["When its costs are greater than its benefits at all, since a DF only matters once the ratio of the two exceeds one.", "When its costs divided by its benefits are at least the disproportion factor, so equality already fails the measure.", "When its costs divided by its benefits are GREATER than the disproportion factor, that is cost > DF x benefit.", "When its ICAF is greater than the VPF, whatever the DF, since the DF is a matter for the ALARP note alone."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The checklist''s test is cost > DF x benefit, strictly greater. A cost merely above its benefit is expected under ALARP, which is why a DF of more than 1 exists. ''At least'' would put equality in the upper band, the convention the engine declined. The ICAF against the VPF ignores the DF and the injury lines, so it is no substitute for the test.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 2, 'The EDIKAN firewall, undiscounted, has a cost of 350000.00 against a benefit of 40000.00. What does the engine return at its stated DF of 3?', '["A ratio of 8.750000, GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, and a largest reasonably practicable cost of 120000.00.", "A ratio of 8.750000, NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because the ratio is compared with a DF of 10 whatever the caller states.", "A ratio of 8.750000, GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, and a largest reasonable cost of 400000.00.", "A ratio of 9.350247, GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, the figure the engine gives at the 2003 checklist limits."]'::jsonb, 0, '350000.00 over 40000.00 is 8.750000, which is greater than 3, so the verdict is GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, and DF 3 times 40000.00 is 120000.00, as the DF sweep prints. The engine compares with the DF the caller states. 400000.00 is the largest reasonable cost at a DF of 10, and 9.350247 is the discounted ratio, which this undiscounted question does not ask for.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 3, 'Along the DF sweep of the undiscounted EDIKAN firewall, at which of the swept factors does the verdict first turn to NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE?', '["At a DF of 5, where the largest reasonable cost of 200000.00 passes the cost.", "At no swept DF at all, because a ratio of 8.750000 is grossly disproportionate at every factor the checklist allows.", "At a DF of 3, where the ratio of 8.750000 falls below three times the benefit and the measure becomes practicable.", "At a DF of 10, where the largest reasonably practicable cost is 400000.00, above the cost of 350000.00."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The sweep prints GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at DF 1, 2, 3 and 5, and NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at DF 10, where 400000.00 exceeds the cost of 350000.00. At DF 5 the largest reasonable cost of 200000.00 is below the cost, so the measure fails there. A ratio of 8.750000 is below a DF of 10, and it is well above 3.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 4, 'Across the DF sweep of the undiscounted firewall, which quantity stays the same at every factor?', '["The largest reasonably practicable cost, 120000.00, because it is fixed by the cost and the benefit alone, whatever the DF.", "The cost to benefit ratio, 8.750000, because the DF moves only the verdict and the largest reasonable cost.", "The verdict, GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because a verdict is a property of the measure whatever factor is applied to it.", "The margin by which the cost exceeds the limit, since the cost and the benefit are fixed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course says the cost to benefit ratio does not depend on the DF; only the verdict and the largest reasonable cost do. The largest reasonable cost runs 40000.00, 80000.00, 120000.00, 200000.00 and 400000.00 down the sweep, and the verdict turns at DF 10. The margin between the cost and that limit shrinks as the limit climbs, although the cost and the benefit themselves stay fixed.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 5, 'What is the largest reasonably practicable cost the engine returns for the undiscounted firewall at a DF of 1, and why that figure?', '["120000.00, because the engine replaces a DF of 1 with the firewall''s stated DF of 3 before it multiplies anything.", "350000.00, the present value of the cost, because at a DF of 1 the limit is simply whatever the measure costs.", "40000.00, DF times the present value of the benefit, which at a DF of 1 is the benefit itself.", "400000.00, the figure at a DF of 10, because a DF of 1 is below the checklist''s range and is lifted to its top."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine returns DF times the present value of the benefit, so at DF 1 it is 40000.00, the benefit. 120000.00 is the figure at DF 3 and 400000.00 at DF 10, each a real sweep row at the wrong factor. The limit is set by the benefit; 350000.00 is the cost that is compared against it.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 6, 'The golden case exactly-at-df gives a cost of 9000.000000000002 against DF 3 times a benefit of 3000.00, which is exactly equal on paper. What does the engine return?', '["NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE with `atBoundary` true, so the measure is still reasonably practicable and the duty to adopt it stands.", "GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because the double precision cost sits a hair above DF times the benefit and the comparison is strict.", "GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE with `atBoundary` true, since a boundary case goes to the band that errs toward rejecting the measure.", "A refusal, because a cost exactly equal to DF times the benefit leaves the test undecided and the analyst must choose a DF."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course shows the golden result: NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, atBoundary true. A cost exactly DF times the benefit belongs to the lower band by the owner''s decision, the 1e-9 snap absorbs the hair in double, and the checklist says "greater than". No verdict rejects a measure at the boundary, and the engine never refuses a case for sitting on it.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 7, 'A caller weighs a measure with a disproportion factor of 0.5. What does the engine return?', '["A verdict at a DF of 1, the engine raising any factor below one to the smallest one the HSE guidance allows it to take.", "A verdict at 0.5, since a DF below one simply asks for a stricter test and the engine applies whatever factor it receives.", "A verdict at a DF of 10, the checklist''s example figure, which the engine applies whenever a stated factor is out of range.", "disproportionFactor: must be 1 or more: HSE, \"DFs that may be considered gross vary from upwards of 1\""]'::jsonb, 3, 'The quoted message is the engine''s own refusal, and it cites HSE''s own wording that DFs vary from upwards of 1. A refusal returns no verdict, so the engine neither lifts the factor to 1, nor applies 0.5, nor substitutes the checklist''s example DF of 10.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 8, 'What does the checklist''s worked example say about a DF above 10, and how does the engine treat one?', '["The example forbids a DF above 10, and the engine refuses any factor above that ceiling in its own words to the caller.", "The example says \"a DF of more than 10 is unlikely\"; the engine accepts it, since it refuses only a DF below 1.", "The example treats 10 as the default DF, which the engine applies to every call that leaves the factor out.", "The example requires a DF above 10 for a major hazard, and the engine lifts a smaller DF to 10."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course quotes the checklist: a DF of more than 10 is unlikely, which is guidance to the analyst. The engine''s only DF refusal is a factor below 1. There is no ceiling refusal, no default DF, and nothing in the course makes 10 a floor for any kind of site.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 9, 'Quoted exactly, how does the engine''s result state the model behind its verdict?', '["grossly disproportionate when cost / benefit >= DF; benefit = (dPLL x VPF + other harms) per year over the life; CPF = cost / (dPLL x years)", "grossly disproportionate when cost / benefit > DF; benefit = dPLL x VPF per year over the life; CPF = cost / (discounted dPLL x years)", "grossly disproportionate when cost / benefit > DF; benefit = (dPLL x VPF + other harms) per year over the life; CPF = cost / (dPLL x years)", "grossly disproportionate when benefit / cost > DF; benefit = (dPLL x VPF + other harms) per year over the life; CPF = cost / dPLL"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course quotes the model string verbatim: strictly greater, other harms inside the benefit, and a CPF over dPLL times the years. The ''>='' string would put equality in the upper band. The second drops the other harms and discounts the fatalities, and the third inverts the ratio and loses the years from the CPF.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 10, 'At the 2003 checklist limits the EDIKAN firewall''s ratio is 9.350247, and under R2P2''s convention 9.328625. What is the verdict at its stated DF of 3 under each?', '["NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE under both, because discounting lowers the cost and the measure then passes at a DF of 3.", "GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at the checklist limits, and NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE under R2P2, the lower of the two ratios.", "NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at the checklist limits, since the checklist is the source of the test and favours the measure.", "GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE under both, as it is undiscounted at 8.750000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course shows GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at DF 3 under all three conventions: every ratio, 8.750000, 9.350247 and 9.328625, is greater than 3. Discounting lowers the benefit too, so the ratio actually rises above its undiscounted value. A slightly lower ratio under R2P2 is still nearly three times the DF, and no source''s test favours a measure.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 11, 'The measure under study prevents no fatality and no injury at all. What does `costBenefit` return, in its own words?', '["deltaPllPerYr: the measure prevents nothing (no PLL reduction and no other harm): there is no benefit to weigh the cost against", "A ratio of zero with the verdict NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, since a measure that costs something and prevents nothing is harmless.", "An infinite ratio, GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, since any cost against a zero benefit is out of proportion.", "A largest reasonably practicable cost of zero, with the verdict left for the analyst to write into the ALARP note in their own words."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A measure that prevents nothing has no benefit to weigh, and the engine refuses it with the message quoted, which is its own. A refusal returns no ratio, no verdict and no largest cost, so an infinite ratio, a zero ratio and a zero limit are all results the engine does not produce.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 12, 'Why does a cost exactly equal to DF times the benefit leave the measure reasonably practicable, in the engine''s own reasoning?', '["Because R2P2''s bias to safety puts every value on a threshold on the side that adopts the measure, which is the engine''s source for the rule.", "Because the checklist''s test is costs over benefits \"greater than\" the DF, and the owner''s decision puts a value at a threshold in the lower band.", "Because the engine rounds the ratio to six decimals before comparing, and a ratio that rounds to the DF is treated as below it.", "Because the engine gives the benefit of the doubt to the duty holder whenever the verdict would otherwise be close to the line."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The checklist words its test as "greater than" the DF, and the owner''s decision is that a value exactly at a threshold belongs to the lower band, which for this test is NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE. R2P2''s bias to safety is named as the argument for the other convention on individual risk. The engine rounds nothing before comparing, and it favours no party.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 13, 'The firewall fails at a factor of two, with 80000.00 as the most it could cost there. How should a duty holder read that figure?', '["That the duty holder should spend 80000.00 on the firewall and no more, adopting a cheaper version of the same measure at that price.", "That 80000.00 is the benefit of the firewall at a DF of 2, since the engine doubles the benefit when a DF of 2 is applied.", "That the firewall would be reasonably practicable at a DF of 2 only if its cost did not exceed 80000.00, while it costs 350000.00.", "That the firewall is GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE by 80000.00, the amount by which its cost exceeds the limit at that factor."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The largest reasonably practicable cost is DF times the present value of the benefit, 2 x 40000.00, the most the measure could cost and still pass. The firewall costs 350000.00, so it fails at DF 2. The figure prescribes no spend, the benefit stays 40000.00 whatever the DF, and the gap to the cost is far larger than 80000.00.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 14, 'An analyst reports the EDIKAN firewall as ''NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at DF 10''. Which statement about that verdict is true?', '["It holds at every DF above 3, since the firewall''s stated DF of 3 is the only factor at which the measure is weighed at all.", "It holds at every DF, since the ratio of 8.750000 is fixed and the verdict follows the ratio alone, whatever factor is chosen.", "It holds at every DF of 5 or more, since a largest reasonable cost of 200000.00 at DF 5 already clears the cost.", "It holds only at a DF of 10; at DF 1, 2, 3 and 5 the same measure is GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The DF sweep shows the verdict turns between DF 5 and DF 10: GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at 1, 2, 3 and 5, and NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at 10. The ratio is fixed, but the verdict compares it with the DF, so it cannot be the same at every factor. 200000.00 is below the cost of 350000.00, so DF 5 does not clear it.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gross-disproportion', 15, 'A measure''s cost exceeds DF times its benefit by the smallest amount the snap does NOT absorb. What happens, and why is the 1e-9 snap still needed at this test?', '["It is GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, strictly greater; the snap exists so a cost equal to DF times the benefit on paper, but a hair above it in double, stays at the boundary.", "It is NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because any cost within one percent of the limit is taken as the limit; the snap is only needed for individual risk bands.", "It is refused, because the engine will not decide a case so close to the line; the snap exists to widen the refusal to one part in a billion.", "It is NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE with `atBoundary` true, because every cost above the limit is snapped back onto it by the 1e-9 rule."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Above the snap, the strictly greater rule applies and the verdict is GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE. The golden exactly-at-df case shows why the snap is needed: 9000.000000000002 is equal on paper to three times 3000.00 and lands a hair above in double, and it stays NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE. The snap is 1e-9 relative, far narrower than one percent, it snaps only values that close, and the engine refuses no case for being near the line.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 1, 'A reviewer asks for an aversion weighted risk integral beside the F-N curve. What does the engine offer, and what does the analyst do instead?', '["An aversion weighted integral with an exponent of 2, the risk averse value the Dutch line carries, applied to every scenario of the curve in turn.", "An aversion weighted integral with an exponent the caller states, since the engine takes every such weighting as an input like any other.", "No aversion weighted integral, since no source it read defines one; the analyst reports the expected value sum of f times N and the F-N curve, and argues aversion in words.", "No integral of any kind, the expected value sum of f times N included, because no published worked F-N example was available to test one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course says the engine has no aversion weighted risk integral because no source it read defines one, and it implements the expected value sum of f times N instead. The alpha of 2 is the slope of a criterion line, which is no weighting of the curve, and the engine takes no weighting input at all. The expected value sum is computed and reported with the curve.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 2, 'An analyst wants the single R2P2 societal point extended into a line so the whole F-N curve can be compared with it. What does the engine provide?', '["The point alone, N = 50 at 0.000200000000 per year with no slope, because R2P2 defers extrapolation to a reference the engine did not read; a line is the analyst''s own.", "A line of slope minus one through the point, as the preset `r2p2-para-136`, because that is the common practice R2P2 prints beside its point and every analyst is expected to apply to the whole curve.", "A line of slope minus two through the point, borrowed from the Dutch line, because the engine carries one risk averse slope for every criterion.", "The point extended flat to every N of 50 or more, since R2P2 asks for 50 or more deaths and a flat line captures exactly that reading."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The preset `r2p2-para-136` is one point, N = 50 at 0.000200000000 per year, and the engine gives it no slope because R2P2 defers extrapolation. A slope of minus one is common practice, but R2P2 does not print it and no preset carries it: an analyst supplies it as their own criterion, C = 0.01 and alpha = 1. The Dutch slope is another criterion''s, and no flat extension exists.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 3, 'An analyst supplies the slope minus one line through the R2P2 point as their own criterion. What does the engine''s basis then say about its source?', '["\"UK HSE R2P2 (2001) para 136\", and the JISIKE curve against it EXCEEDS, worst ratio 18.000000 at N = 300.000000.", "\"TNO Purple Book CPR 18E (1999) Figure 6.8\", and the JISIKE curve against it is BELOW, worst ratio 0.001000 at N = 50.000000.", "\"criterion as given\", and the JISIKE curve TOUCHES it, the corner at N = 10 sitting on the line within the snap of 1e-9.", "\"criterion as given\", and the JISIKE off-site curve against it is BELOW, worst ratio 0.014910 at N = 3.000000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A line the analyst supplies carries the source "criterion as given", and the JISIKE off-site curve against C = 0.01, alpha = 1 is BELOW with a worst ratio of 0.014910 at N = 3.000000. 18.000000 at N = 300 is the worst ratio against the Dutch line, 0.001000 at N = 50 is the ratio to the R2P2 point itself, and the JISIKE curve has no corner at N = 10.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 4, 'The Dutch line `vrom-establishments` cites Bevi. What does the engine not know about it, and how should an analyst treat the line?', '["Bevi''s successor keeps the same three points, so the analyst treats the line as the current Dutch legal limit for establishments across the Netherlands.", "Bevi was repealed on 1 January 2024 and its successor was not read, so the analyst treats the line as a historical published comparison.", "Bevi''s successor moved the line to a slope of minus one, so the analyst should switch to a caller line with C = 0.01 at once for every establishment.", "Bevi exists only as an unpublished draft, so the analyst treats the three points as the engine''s own estimate of the Dutch values."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course says Bevi was repealed on 1 January 2024 and the engine did not read its successor, so the analyst treats the Dutch line as a historical published comparison. Nothing is known about the successor''s points or slope, so neither claim about it can be made. The three Bevi points are published and lie on the Purple Book line.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 5, 'Which of these is a published, reproduced check on the societal side of the engine, where the F-N curve itself has no published worked example?', '["The F-N curve of the JISIKE off-site scenarios, reproduced from a CCPS worked example that prints the same five scenarios.", "The area identity, 0.000336000000 per year, which reproduces a worked societal calculation printed in the Purple Book.", "The criterion side: the three Bevi points lie on the Purple Book line, and the R2P2 point is printed in its source.", "The Fd fractions indoors and outdoors, reproduced against the numeric example that the Purple Book prints beside Table 5.3."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course says what IS published and reproduced is the criterion side: the Bevi points on the line and the printed R2P2 point. The CCPS and HSE worked examples were not available. The area identity is a self-consistency check with no published source behind it, and the Purple Book prints no numeric example for the Fd fractions.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 6, 'No published worked F-N example was available to the engine. How is its F-N curve checked, and what may an ALARP note therefore claim?', '["By self-consistency only, a brute force count at every corner, a second route on a grid of N and the area identity, so the note never cites an F-N figure as published-verified.", "By comparison with the Purple Book''s own worked societal risk figure, so the note may cite each corner of the curve as published-verified in the societal section of the ALARP note.", "By comparison with the Dutch line, since a curve that lies on the Bevi points is by that fact reproduced against a published source.", "It is not checked at all, so the note must leave the societal picture out and report the individual risk of the most exposed person only."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course lists the three self-consistency checks and says it never presents an F-N figure as a published reproduction. The Purple Book prints no worked F-N curve. A criterion line is a comparison, which verifies nothing about the curve''s own arithmetic. The curve is checked, so the societal picture belongs in the note, with its checks stated.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 7, 'What does the area identity check on the JISIKE off-site curve, and what does it return?', '["That F at each corner times its own N of deaths sums to the PLL of the JISIKE crew: 0.002420000000 on both sides of the identity.", "That the frequency of scenarios with no deaths equals the area under the curve: 0.000050000000 on both sides of it.", "That the curve''s highest corner equals the sum of every scenario frequency in the set: 0.000049700000 on both sides of the identity.", "That F at each corner times the width of its step sums to the expected fatalities per year: 0.000336000000 on both sides."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The area under the step curve, F at each corner times the width of its step, is 0.000336000000 per year, equal to the expected fatalities per year, which is the off-site PLL. 0.002420000000 is the crew PLL, a different set of scenarios. The no-death frequency is kept out of the curve, and 0.000049700000 is F(3), which leaves out the scenario that reaches no one.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 8, 'Purple Book Table 4.5 cells, the 0.6 and 0.4 vapour cloud split and the Fd factors are all in the engine. What does the engine not know about them?', '["Their units: each is exported without one, so the analyst converts them to per year before a lookup is quoted in an ALARP note beside the stated frequencies of the tree.", "A check against a second reading: each is one transcription with no published example to test it, so the analyst states the values used and their source.", "Their price year: each was set in 1999 and must be uprated by the benefit growth rate before it is used in a gross disproportion test of any measure the analyst weighs.", "Their owner: each belongs to the facilities courses, so the analyst must fetch the values from there and never state them."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course says each is a single transcription with no published example to test it, and the analyst states the values used and their source; a capstone states every ignition probability and the split for the same reason. They are probabilities and fractions, which carry no per year unit and no price year. They are part of this engine, stated by the analyst, and no other course supplies them.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 9, 'The engine''s own validation planted a wrong clothing factor in both the engine and its oracle, and the suite stayed green. What does the course conclude about the Fd fractions?', '["That the clothing factor is wrong today, so every Fd figure the engine returns must be corrected by hand before it enters an ALARP note as the input for a population cell.", "That the fractions should be applied to individual risk as a vulnerability factor, where a wrong value matters less than in societal risk.", "That it teaches the rules and never grades a number that passes through them, because a check shared by engine and oracle cannot catch a shared transcription.", "That the suite is broken, so the analyst runs the oracle alone and quotes its fractions in place of the engine''s figures."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course records the planted factor as the reason it never grades a number that passes through the Fd rules: a wrong value shared by the engine and its oracle agrees with itself. It says nothing is wrong with today''s factor. The fractions are for societal risk and are not a vulnerability factor, and the oracle shares the same transcription, so it is no escape.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 10, 'Where does every probability of death this engine uses come from, and what does the engine do about it?', '["From consequence modelling in the consequence course; the engine takes Pd as a stated input and the analyst records where it came from.", "From the Purple Book Table 5.3 fractions, which the engine converts to a probability of death at every place on the plot plan that the analyst is given.", "From the event tree, whose leaf probabilities are each a probability of death once the tree has been multiplied through from its root to the leaf.", "From the risk matrix, which this course never scores, but whose category the engine converts to a probability of death."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course says a probability of death is the output of consequence modelling in the consequence course, and this course takes it as a stated input and records its source. Table 5.3 gives fractions of a population indoors. A leaf probability is the probability of a path, never of death. The risk matrix belongs to the risk and change course, and no matrix category becomes a Pd here.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 11, 'A full assessment needs a grid of points, a wind rose and a population map. What does the engine do with them?', '["It builds the grid from the plot plan and reads the wind rose from a stated weather file, returning an LSIR at every grid point.", "It spreads each scenario''s frequency across twelve wind sectors, the Appendix 6.B default, before it sums the LSIR at a place.", "It takes the population map as an input and returns the Fd fractions per cell as a vulnerability factor for individual risk.", "Nothing: the caller supplies each scenario frequency and Pd, so the analyst does that bookkeeping and records it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course says the engine does no grid or wind rose bookkeeping; the caller supplies each scenario frequency and probability of death. The 12 wind sectors of Appendix 6.B are that worked example''s stated inputs, which are no engine default. The Fd fractions are for societal risk, never a vulnerability factor for individual risk.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 12, 'The Purple Book limits exposure to a fire to 20 s and to a toxic cloud to 30 minutes. What does the engine do when an exposure runs longer?', '["It refuses the call, naming the exposure field, because an exposure beyond the Purple Book''s limit is outside the range the engine accepts.", "It applies the cap without refusing and states the time it used, so a toxic exposure of 60 minutes is computed at 30, which is consequence modelling on the other side of the seam.", "It computes at the full exposure and adds a warning, leaving the analyst to decide whether the Purple Book cap should be applied by hand.", "It scales the probability of death down in proportion, so a toxic exposure of 60 minutes counts at half its stated probability of death."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course says the engine applies the cap without refusing and states the time used: a toxic exposure of 60 minutes is computed at 30, and the toxic case belongs to the consequence side of the seam. No refusal is raised, the cap is applied by the engine itself, and nothing rescales a stated probability of death.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 13, 'Purple Book Appendix 6.B prints Pd = 0.381, where the whole chain, as the golden records it, gives 0.380294556093. What explains the gap, and what should a reproduction do?', '["The engine''s chain is wrong in its third decimal, so a reproduction quotes the printed 0.381 and discards the engine''s figure for the step and every later step built on it.", "The source used R = 360.555 m where the engine uses 361 m, so a reproduction replaces the engine''s distance with the printed one.", "The source is internally rounded, since 0.381 follows only from its rounded ECW of 86.2 m; a reproduction that needs a rounded intermediate says which one.", "The gap comes from the probit, a stated input that belongs to the consequence course, so the gap is left unexplained in the note."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course says the whole chain gives 0.380294556093, which rounds to 0.380, and the printed 0.381 follows only from the rounded ECW of 86.2; both routes reproduce the printed contribution of 7e-9 to two significant figures. The gap describes the SOURCE. It is the source that uses R = 361 m for a point at 360.555 m, and the gap is fully explained.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 14, 'Which of these does the engine decline to do, by its own design?', '["Return an LSIR for a place whose probability of death is zero for some scenario, which it refuses as an incomplete input set of scenarios at that place.", "Compare an F-N curve with the single R2P2 point, since a point without a slope cannot be compared with a step curve at any corner of it.", "Accept a VPF above the 2003 checklist figure, since any value higher than 1336800 lies outside the HSE illustrative range.", "Re-grade anything the consequence course or the facilities courses own: it exports none of their functions, and the jest suite asserts so."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course lists re-grading nothing the consequence or facilities courses own among what the engine does not do: it exports none of their functions. A zero Pd simply contributes zero, the curve is compared with the R2P2 point at N = 50, and a VPF is refused only when it is missing or not above 0.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-what-the-engine-does-not-know', 15, 'An analyst wants to apply the Purple Book indoor fractions as a vulnerability factor on the EREMOR operator''s individual risk. What does the engine''s position say?', '["No source the engine read gives a vulnerability factor for individual risk; the indoor fractions are for societal risk, so the default is 1 and the result returns the factor used beside each place''s contribution.", "The indoor fractions are the Purple Book''s vulnerability factor, day 0.93 and night 0.99, and the engine applies them by default to every place.", "The engine refuses any vulnerability factor below 1, since only the Purple Book fractions may lower an individual risk and they are not allowed here.", "The engine computes a vulnerability factor from the operator''s hours, so the analyst supplies no factor and the fractions are never used."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course says no source the engine read gives a vulnerability factor for individual risk, the Purple Book indoor and outdoor fractions are for societal risk, the default is 1, and the basis records what was supplied. The fractions are no default factor. A factor from 0 to 1 is accepted when the analyst supplies one, and the engine derives nothing from hours beyond the occupancy.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 1, 'The EREMOR supervisor''s IRPA is 0.000008743500 per year. Banded against the worker preset, what does the engine say, and what follows for the duty holder?', '["BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, because it lies more than a hundred times below the worker upper limit, so no demonstration is needed at all.", "TOLERABLE, 8.743500 times the lower limit, so an ALARP demonstration is required before the individual risk can be accepted.", "TOLERABLE, 20.000000 times the lower limit, the ratio the engine returns for every individual risk in the middle region.", "UNACCEPTABLE, because a supervisor is a member of the public while in the accommodation, and the public limit is exceeded there."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course bands the supervisor at TOLERABLE, 8.743500 times the lower limit of 1e-6, and says an ALARP demonstration is required. Being well below the upper limit is not being broadly acceptable. 20.000000 is the ratio for 2e-5 per year, since the ratio moves with the individual risk, and a worker on site is banded as a worker in every place of the roster.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 2, 'An ALARP note must band the individual risk of the most exposed person. For EREMOR, which figure is that, and why that one?', '["The process deck LSIR, 0.000148150000 per year, because the note bands a place and the deck is the most dangerous place on the installation.", "The supervisor''s IRPA, 0.000008743500 per year, because the supervisor is the senior person on the roster and the note bands them first.", "The LSIRs summed with no occupancy, 0.000154803000 per year, because the most exposed person is assumed to be everywhere at once.", "The operator''s IRPA, 0.000017541379 per year, because it is what one person carries over the places occupied, and it exceeds the supervisor''s 0.000008743500."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The note bands a person''s IRPA, and the operator''s 0.000017541379 is higher than the supervisor''s 0.000008743500. An LSIR assumes someone present at the place all the time, outdoors and unprotected, which describes no one on the roster. Seniority is irrelevant, and summing the LSIRs with no occupancy has one person in three places at once.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 3, 'The EDIKAN verdict turns on choices the analyst makes. What does an ALARP demonstration do about that?', '["It states each choice and why (the discounting convention, the DF, the VPF and its year) and shows the verdict at the other defensible choices.", "It picks the convention and DF that give the most favourable verdict for the duty holder, and records that choice in the ALARP note.", "It quotes the undiscounted verdict only, since the checklist example is undiscounted and every other convention is a matter of opinion for the reader.", "It averages the three conventions'' ratios and compares the average with the DF, so that no single choice decides the verdict."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course says an ALARP demonstration states each choice and why, and shows the verdict at the other defensible choices. Choosing for the most favourable verdict is not a method. Rates are inputs with two HSE conventions behind them, so the undiscounted row alone hides the choice, and no source averages the conventions.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 4, 'Which of these does the ALARP note, in the shape this course grades at Expert, leave out?', '["The ICAF of each measure set beside the VPF the analyst chose, with its year and its source as the checklist and R2P2 name it.", "What the engine did not know, such as current HSE values or a published F-N worked example for the societal picture of the site.", "A score from a risk matrix, which this course never scores, since the matrix belongs to the risk and change course.", "Each measure''s deltaPLL, costs, life, VPF, other harms, discounting convention and DF, each with its source as the note states it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course''s ALARP note carries the individual risk of the most exposed person and its region, the societal picture if warranted, each measure with its inputs and sources, the ratio and verdict, the ICAF beside the VPF, the measures adopted and rejected, and what the engine did not know. A risk matrix score is not part of it: the matrix belongs to the risk and change course.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 5, 'An individual risk comes back TOLERABLE. Is it acceptable?', '["Yes, since TOLERABLE is the engine''s word for acceptable, and the demonstration flag is advisory only for the duty holder.", "Not yet: an individual risk in the TOLERABLE region is not acceptable until the ALARP demonstration is made.", "Yes, if it lies below the public upper limit of 1e-4, which every worker individual risk must also meet on site.", "No, never, since a TOLERABLE result must be reduced until it is BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE before work continues on the installation."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course says an individual risk in the TOLERABLE region is not acceptable until the demonstration is made; the engine sets `alarpDemonstrationRequired` there. TOLERABLE is no synonym for acceptable, the public limit is not the worker test, and ALARP asks for reduction as low as reasonably practicable, which need not reach the broadly acceptable region.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 6, 'The EDIKAN firewall is GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at its stated DF of 3 under every convention. How does the ALARP note treat a measure rejected on cost?', '["It drops the measure from the note entirely, since a grossly disproportionate measure has no further bearing on the ALARP case or on the duty holder''s decision.", "It raises the DF to 10 so that the measure passes, since the checklist allows a DF up to that figure for any measure it covers.", "It records the measure as adopted in part, spending the largest reasonably practicable cost of 120000.00 on a cheaper version.", "It lists it among the measures rejected as grossly disproportionate, and still weighs it against good practice, which the engine does not know."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course''s note lists the measures adopted and the ones rejected as grossly disproportionate, and says a measure rejected on cost is still weighed against good practice, which the engine does not know. Dropping it hides the decision. The DF is a stated choice to be justified, never tuned to pass a measure, and the largest reasonable cost is a limit, which prescribes no spend.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 7, 'Each measure in the ALARP note carries its deltaPLL, costs, life, VPF, other harms, discounting convention and DF. Why must the note give a source for every one of them?', '["Because the engine checks each figure against its source before it computes, and refuses any input that arrives with no source attached.", "Because a reviewer needs the sources to repeat the arithmetic by hand, the engine''s figures being illustrative until they are checked.", "Because each is a stated input the engine never invents, so the verdict is only as defensible as the source behind each figure.", "Because only the VPF and the DF are stated inputs, and the sources show that the other figures were computed by the engine itself."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine invents no number: deltaPLL, VPF, DF, the costs, the life and every rate are inputs, so the note''s defence of a verdict rests on where each came from. The engine takes no source field and refuses nothing for lacking one. Its arithmetic is exact and needs no hand check, and every one of these figures is stated by the analyst.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 8, 'Undiscounted, the EDIKAN ICAF of 8750000.00 over the VPF of 1000000 equals the ratio of 8.750000. At the 2003 checklist limits the ICAF of 8026550.41 and the ratio of 9.350247 differ. Why?', '["Because the ICAF divides the discounted cost by fatalities prevented counted UNDISCOUNTED, while the ratio divides it by a benefit that is discounted.", "Because the checklist limits discount the fatalities prevented at 0.015, which lowers the ICAF below the VPF times the ratio whatever the life of the measure being weighed.", "Because the ICAF leaves out the injury lines, and the EDIKAN firewall carries injury lines that only the ratio''s benefit counts.", "Because the ratio is computed at the DF of 3 while the ICAF is formed at a DF of 1, so the two are never meant to agree."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With no injuries and zero rates, the benefit is deltaPLL times the life times the VPF, so the ICAF over the VPF equals the ratio. At the checklist limits the cost is discounted in both, but the benefit is discounted and the fatalities prevented stay at 0.040000, so the two part company. The fatalities are never discounted, the firewall states no injury lines, and neither figure involves the DF.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 9, 'The note''s societal picture for the JISIKE off-site curve reports it against the Dutch line and against the R2P2 point. What does each comparison return, and with what caution?', '["BELOW against both, with a worst ratio of 0.014910 at N = 3.000000, since the Dutch line and the R2P2 point are the same criterion drawn two ways.", "EXCEEDS against both, with a worst ratio of 18.000000 at N = 300.000000, since the R2P2 point is extended along the Dutch slope of minus two.", "TOUCHES against the Dutch line at N = 10 and EXCEEDS against the R2P2 point at N = 50, so the note reports the curve as marginal overall.", "EXCEEDS with a worst ratio of 18.000000 at N = 300.000000 against the Dutch line, a historical comparison since Bevi''s repeal, and BELOW at 0.001000 against the R2P2 point."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course shows EXCEEDS, worst ratio 18.000000 at N = 300 against the Dutch line, and BELOW at 0.001000 against the R2P2 point, which has no slope. The Dutch line is a historical published comparison because Bevi was repealed. 0.014910 is the worst ratio against the analyst''s own slope minus one line, and the TOUCHES case is a different curve built to sit on the line.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 10, 'R2P2 says its individual risk limits are guidelines. How should an ALARP note use the band the engine returns?', '["As a legal limit: a band word is a verdict in its own right, and the note needs nothing beyond it to settle the ALARP case for the installation and its people.", "As a guideline applied with judgement: the band says where the individual risk sits, and the demonstration and the note carry the judgement.", "As a starting point only, to be replaced by a risk matrix score, which the risk and change course owns and which settles the case for the duty holder.", "As an estimate the analyst may round to the nearest band edge before quoting it, since the limits are only guidelines."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course says R2P2''s limits are guidelines, to be applied with judgement, and the note carries the judgement: the region, the measures weighed and what the engine did not know. A band word alone settles nothing. The risk matrix belongs to the risk and change course and this course never scores one, and rounding a figure toward a band edge is the opposite of computing from the inputs.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 11, 'An analyst weighs the EDIKAN firewall at a VPF of 1000000. What must the ALARP note say about that choice?', '["Nothing, since 1000000 is the engine''s default VPF and a default needs no justification in the note beside the verdict.", "That 1000000 is the current HSE value, which every ALARP note in the United Kingdom is required to apply to a fatality prevented by the measure.", "State the VPF and its year, 2001 prices from R2P2, and justify it, since the engine has no default and current HSE values were not found.", "That the 2003 figure of 1336800 was rejected as too high, since the lower VPF always gives the more cautious verdict on a measure being weighed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course says the analyst states the VPF and its year and justifies it: the engine has no default VPF, and current HSE figures were not found on a live page, so neither exported value is a recommendation. A lower VPF lowers the benefit, which makes a measure look worse value, so it is the less cautious choice for the people protected.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 12, 'Which line belongs in the ''what the engine did not know'' section of an ALARP note that also carries a societal picture?', '["That no published F-N worked example was available, so the curve rests on self-consistency checks and no F-N figure is cited as published-verified.", "That the engine could not compute a PLL for scenarios whose N is not a whole number, so fractional deaths were rounded before the curve was built from them.", "That the engine could not band an individual risk exactly at a threshold, so the analyst assigned it to the lower band by hand in the note.", "That the engine could not discount a benefit growing at a rate, so the R2P2 convention was approximated by the undiscounted row."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course lists no published F-N worked example among what the engine does not know, and the analyst never cites an F-N figure as published-verified. The other three describe gaps that do not exist: N need not be whole, a threshold value is banded by the engine''s own lower band rule, and the engine takes a benefit growth rate as an input.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 13, 'A draft note argues that construction workers in the R2P2 box carried 0.000046646142 per year, so the EREMOR operator''s 0.000017541379 needs no ALARP demonstration. What is wrong with the argument?', '["Nothing is wrong: an individual risk below a published industry rate is broadly acceptable by comparison, which is how R2P2 builds its box of rates.", "The construction rate is a public figure and must be banded against `r2p2-public`, where it is UNACCEPTABLE and so no comparison can be drawn.", "The operator''s figure is UNACCEPTABLE against the worker preset, so the note should skip the demonstration and stop the work at once.", "Both figures are TOLERABLE against the worker preset, and a published industry rate in the same region makes nothing acceptable, so the demonstration is still required."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Against the worker preset both lie between 1e-6 and 1e-3, so both are TOLERABLE, and a TOLERABLE individual risk is not acceptable until the demonstration is made. The box shows where industry rates sit; it lowers no one''s duty. The construction rate is a worker rate banded against the worker preset, and 0.000017541379 is far below the upper limit of 1e-3.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 14, 'An analyst has banded the individual risk, weighed each measure and written the verdicts. Which item of the ALARP note do the numbers alone NOT settle?', '["Whether the most exposed person''s IRPA lies in the TOLERABLE region, which the engine cannot tell from the thresholds that it is given.", "Whether a measure rejected on cost is still required as good practice, which the engine does not know and the analyst must weigh.", "Whether the cost to benefit ratio exceeds the DF, which the engine leaves to the analyst to compute by hand after the call has returned.", "Whether the ICAF is formed over undiscounted fatalities, which the engine leaves as a choice for the analyst in each call to its cost function."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course says a measure rejected on cost is still weighed against good practice, which the engine does not know. The engine bands the individual risk against the stated thresholds, returns the ratio and the verdict, and always forms the ICAF over undiscounted fatalities prevented, which is its declared choice.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-judgement-end-to-end', 15, 'The EDIKAN firewall passes only at a DF of 10 in the undiscounted sweep. What must a note that adopts it at DF 10 add?', '["Nothing further, since the checklist''s example uses a DF of 10 and that figure therefore needs no justification in any ALARP note.", "A statement that the ratio of 8.750000 was reduced by the DF to below one, which is the condition the checklist sets for adoption of a measure.", "Why a DF of 10 is justified, since DFs vary from upwards of 1, a DF above 10 is unlikely, and the verdict turns between DF 5 and DF 10.", "The discounted verdict at DF 10, since only a discounted verdict counts and the undiscounted sweep is shown to the reader for interest only."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course says the verdict can turn on the DF, and a demonstration states each choice and why: DFs vary from upwards of 1, the checklist''s example says above 10 is unlikely, and EDIKAN is GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at DF 5 and NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at DF 10. The ratio is never divided by the DF, and the undiscounted row is a defensible convention in its own right.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'A worker''s individual risk of 1e-2 per year is banded against `r2p2-workers`. Why does the engine set `alarpDemonstrationRequired` to false?', '["Because the value is far above the upper limit, so the engine treats the result as an input error for the caller to review and leaves every flag at its default.", "Because a demonstration is only required at or below the lower limit, where the engine asks the analyst to confirm the individual risk.", "Because the value is UNACCEPTABLE whatever the benefit, so there is no ALARP case to demonstrate; the flag is set only in the TOLERABLE region.", "Because a worker''s individual risk never carries an ALARP demonstration, which R2P2 reserves for members of the public off the site."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course shows 1e-2 per year as UNACCEPTABLE for workers with the flag false: above the upper limit an individual risk is unacceptable whatever the benefit, and the demonstration belongs to the TOLERABLE region between the limits. The engine bands the value normally, the flag is false in the broadly acceptable region too, and workers in the middle region do need a demonstration.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'Down the ladder of stated individual risks from 1e-2 to 0 per year, which rows get a different band word from the two R2P2 presets?', '["Only 1e-3 and 5e-4, each TOLERABLE for workers and UNACCEPTABLE for the public; every other rung gets the same word from both.", "Every rung from 1e-3 down to 1e-6, since the two presets share no limit and so disagree across the whole middle of the ladder.", "Only 1e-4, TOLERABLE for workers and UNACCEPTABLE for the public, since it is the public upper limit and the snap puts it above.", "Only 1e-6 and 5e-7, BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE for workers and TOLERABLE for the public, whose lower limit sits below the workers'' one."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The presets share the lower limit of 1e-6 and differ only in the upper limit, 1e-3 for workers and 1e-4 for the public, so only rungs above 1e-4 and at or below 1e-3 are banded differently: 1e-3 and 5e-4. At 1e-4 both return TOLERABLE, the public result naming the boundary it sits on, and 1e-6 and below are BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE under both.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'A public individual risk is computed as 0.1 x 0.1 x 0.01, which is 0.00010000000000000002 in double. What does the engine return against `r2p2-public`?', '["UNACCEPTABLE, with the boundary null, because the product lies a hair above the public limit of 1e-4 per year in IEEE double.", "BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, because a product of three probabilities is read as a probability and falls far below one in a million.", "TOLERABLE, with the boundary null, because the snap moves the value just below the limit so that no boundary is touched.", "TOLERABLE, with the boundary `unacceptable`."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The product lies within 1e-9 relative of the public upper limit, so the snap takes it as the limit, and the limit belongs to the lower band: TOLERABLE with `atBoundary` naming `unacceptable`. Without the snap it would read UNACCEPTABLE. The product is an individual risk per year, and the snap does not move the value off the limit; it names the boundary the value sits on.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'Two stated factors, 0.2 and 0.000005, give 0.0000010000000000000002 when multiplied in IEEE arithmetic. Which band and boundary follow for a member of the public?', '["TOLERABLE, with the boundary `broadly-acceptable`, since the product lies a hair above the limit and the snap only records how close it came.", "BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, with the boundary `broadly-acceptable`, since the snap takes it as the lower limit and a value at the lower limit is broadly acceptable.", "TOLERABLE, with the boundary null, since a value above the lower limit in double is above it in every sense the engine recognises.", "BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE, with the boundary null, since the engine rounds a product of stated factors to twelve decimals before banding it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course''s third snap row: 0.2 x 0.000005 lands a hair above 1e-6 in double, the 1e-9 relative snap takes it as the limit, and the limit is at or below, so the band is BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE with the boundary named. Without the snap it would read TOLERABLE. The engine names the boundary because it snapped, and it rounds nothing before banding.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'Of the five R2P2 box rates the course bands, each against the preset its source names, how many come back TOLERABLE?', '["None: R2P2 says worker rates are normally well below the upper limit, so each of the five comes back BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE against its preset.", "Five: every published industry rate lies between the limits of its preset, which is why R2P2 prints them together in one box of rates.", "Four: agriculture, construction, mining and quarrying, and the service sector, all against the worker preset; only the public gas rate is BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE.", "Three: agriculture, construction, and mining and quarrying, with the service sector and the public gas rate both BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course bands agriculture 0.000077017868, construction 0.000046646142, mining and quarrying 0.000068662455 and the service sector 0.000002573572 TOLERABLE against workers, and the public gas rate 0.000000662252 BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE against the public preset. Well below the upper limit is still above the lower one, and the service sector is still above 1 in a million.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'Agriculture''s 1999/00 fatality rate, 1 in 12 984, is written 0.000077017868 per year. What does R2P2 say of worker rates like it, and what band follows?', '["R2P2 says worker rates are \"normally well below the upper limit\" of 1 in 1000, and the engine returns TOLERABLE against `r2p2-workers`.", "R2P2 says such rates are broadly acceptable for a hazardous industry, and the engine returns BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE against the worker preset.", "R2P2 says such rates exceed what workers should carry, and the engine returns UNACCEPTABLE against the worker preset of 1e-4 per year.", "R2P2 says nothing of the rates it prints, and the engine refuses to band a published statistic, which is not an individual risk per year."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The golden cites the R2P2 para 128 box: worker rates "normally well below the upper limit" of 1 in 1000, and the engine bands 0.000077017868 TOLERABLE. It is above the lower limit of 1e-6, so it is not broadly acceptable, the worker upper limit is 1e-3, and the engine bands the rate like any individual risk per year.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'What model string does `alarpBand` write into its basis, verbatim?', '["UNACCEPTABLE if IR >= upper; BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE if IR < lower; TOLERABLE between", "UNACCEPTABLE if IR > upper; BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE if IR < lower; TOLERABLE (reduce ALARP) otherwise", "UNACCEPTABLE if IR >= upper; BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE if IR <= lower; TOLERABLE (reduce ALARP) between", "UNACCEPTABLE if IR > upper; BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE if IR <= lower; TOLERABLE (reduce ALARP) between"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The course quotes the basis model verbatim, and its two comparisons carry the lower band convention: strictly greater than the upper limit is unacceptable, at or below the lower limit is broadly acceptable. Each distractor moves a threshold value into the upper band at one limit or both, the convention the engine declined.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'Zero per year goes into `alarpBand` under the worker preset. Which band comes back, and why does `atBoundary` stay null?', '["BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE with the boundary `broadly-acceptable`, because every value at or below the lower limit touches that limit.", "BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE with the boundary null, because a boundary is named only when the value sits on a threshold, and 0 sits on neither.", "A refusal, because an individual risk of zero means no scenario was supplied for that preset, and the engine will not band an empty assessment.", "TOLERABLE with the boundary null, because a zero individual risk cannot be demonstrated and so must stay in the middle region."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course''s last row bands 0 as BROADLY_ACCEPTABLE with boundary null under both presets. `atBoundary` names a threshold only when the value sits on it within the snap, and 0 is far from 1e-6. The engine accepts an individual risk of 0 or more, and a zero sits at or below the lower limit.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'Slight injuries in the HSE example: 0.002 cases a year, 300 each, for 25 years. How much benefit, and does the printed figure match?', '["15.00, printed 15, so the print agrees.", "512.50, printed 512, so the print disagrees by the half unit that the source rounded away from the total.", "15.00 a year, which the checklist then carries over 25 years, so the print of 15 for the whole line understates it.", "2072.00, printed 2072, the permanent incapacity line, whose value per case the checklist table mistypes."]'::jsonb, 0, '0.002 x 300 x 25 is 15.00 and the checklist prints 15. 512.50 printed 512 is the serious injury line. The benefit line is a sum over all 25 years, so 15.00 is the whole line and the print of 15 covers the whole life. 2072.00 is the permanent incapacity line, whose table value is the one the checklist mistypes.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'Which lines of the checklist example does the engine reproduce exactly as printed, and where does the source''s rounding show?', '["Every line agrees exactly, and the rounding shows only in the largest reasonable cost, 92835.00 printed 93000, which the source rounded on purpose.", "No line agrees exactly, because the engine discounts the benefit at 0.015 while the checklist leaves the example undiscounted over its 25 years.", "Fatalities 6684, permanent incapacity 2072 and slight injury 15 agree exactly; serious injury 512.50 is printed 512, which carries into the total 9283.50 printed 9283.", "Fatalities and permanent incapacity agree; serious and slight injury are both printed rounded, which is why the total falls short of 9283.50."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course''s table prints 6684.00, 2072.00 and 15.00 against printed 6684, 2072 and 15, and 512.50 against 512, so the total 9283.50 is printed 9283; the limit 92835.00 printed 93000 is a further rounding. The example is undiscounted in both, and the slight injury line is exact.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'The 2003 checklist fatality value the engine exports carries a note in its source string. What does it say?', '["Fatality GBP 1,336,800, to be uprated each year at the 4 percent growth that R2P2 applies to every benefit.", "Fatality GBP 1,336,800, to be applied as a default whenever a caller leaves the VPF out of a call to the engine.", "Fatality GBP 1,336,800 for workers, with a separate public value of 1000000 at the prices of 2001 for the public.", "Fatality GBP 1,336,800 (times 2 for cancer)."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The export''s source string, quoted verbatim in the course, reads: UK HSE, Cost Benefit Analysis (CBA) checklist (2003 values): fatality GBP 1,336,800 (times 2 for cancer). The 4 percent uprating is R2P2''s convention, VPF has no default, and the 2001 figure is R2P2''s VPF at 2001 prices, no public value.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'In what currency does `costBenefit` return its present values, its largest reasonably practicable cost and its ICAF?', '["GBP at 2003 Q3 prices, since the engine converts every input in a call to the checklist''s price basis before computing anything at all.", "The currency of the inputs: the engine converts nothing, so the VPF and the costs must be stated in one currency at one price basis.", "GBP at 2001 prices, since the R2P2 VPF of 1000000 is the base against which the engine scales every other money input.", "US dollars, since the economics engine that supplies the year-end present value works in one reporting currency only."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course''s units table gives VPF and costs in the currency of the inputs, and the basis says the same. The two HSE figures are GBP at their price year and are illustrations only, and the engine converts nothing, so an analyst mixing currencies or price years would get a meaningless ratio.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'The EDIKAN firewall''s deltaPLL is 2e-3. What is that quantity, in this course''s vocabulary?', '["A reduction of 2e-3 expected fatalities per year in the PLL, which is never a probability.", "A probability of 2e-3 that the firewall prevents a death in any one year of its twenty year life, taken as a chance.", "A reduction of 2e-3 per year in one worker''s individual risk, which the engine would band against the worker preset.", "A fraction of 2e-3 of the facility''s fatalities, which the firewall prevents in every year in which it stays in service."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course''s vocabulary says PLL is expected fatalities per year and never a probability, and deltaPLL is the reduction in it that a measure delivers. It is a population quantity, so it is no one person''s individual risk, and it is an expected number per year, which is no share of anything.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'The engine builds the benefit over a measure''s life from each year''s benefit B. How does a benefit growth rate enter, when one is given?', '["It replaces the benefit discount rate, so a growth rate of 0.04 is applied in place of the 0.06 that R2P2 would discount at.", "It multiplies the VPF once at year 0, so the benefit starts higher and every later year is discounted from that level.", "It uprates each year''s benefit, and the uprated flows are then put through the year-end present value at the benefit rate.", "It is subtracted from the cost rate, so that costs and benefits are discounted at the same net rate throughout the life."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course says the benefit over the life is the present value of B each year, uprated by a growth rate if one is given, and R2P2''s convention uses both a 0.06 discount and a 0.04 uprating. The growth rate replaces no rate, it acts on every year''s benefit, and it never touches the cost side.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'Whatever the convention, which part of the EDIKAN firewall''s cost keeps its full value in the present value of the cost?', '["The annual cost of 5000, because a recurring cost is never discounted under either of the two HSE conventions.", "None of it, because at the checklist limits the whole cost of 350000.00 is discounted at 0.035 from the first year.", "The first year''s annual cost only, because the engine places the first year''s flow at year 0 alongside the capital.", "The capital of 250000, because it is spent at year 0."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Capital is spent at year 0, so it is never discounted, and each annual cost of 5000 falls at the end of years 1 to 20 and is discounted. That is why the cost moves only from 350000.00 to 321062.02 at the checklist limits. The first annual flow falls at the end of year 1, never at year 0.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'At the checklist limits the firewall''s ratio rises from 8.750000 to 9.350247, although costs are discounted at the higher rate. Why?', '["Because the benefit is discounted at 0.035 and the cost at 0.015 at the checklist limits, so the benefit shrinks faster.", "Because the capital at year 0 is never discounted, the cost falls proportionally less than the benefit, whose every flow is discounted.", "Because the engine counts the fatalities prevented at a discount at the checklist limits, which lowers the benefit a second time over the life.", "Because the checklist limits add a growth rate on costs, so the cost rises while the benefit is discounted at 0.015."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cost falls from 350000.00 to 321062.02 and the benefit from 40000.00 to 34337.28: most of the cost is the capital at year 0, which carries no discount, while every year of benefit is discounted. The rates are 0.015 on benefits and 0.035 on costs, the fatalities prevented are never discounted, and the checklist row has no growth rate.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'An analyst divides the firewall''s present value of the benefit at the checklist limits, 34337.28, by its VPF and gets 0.034337. What is that figure, and does the engine use it?', '["The discounted fatalities prevented, a derived figure the engine never uses: its ICAF divides by the undiscounted 0.040000.", "The fatalities prevented that the engine divides the firewall''s cost by at the checklist limits, which is why its ICAF is 8026550.41.", "The cost to benefit ratio at the checklist limits, expressed per unit of VPF, which the engine reports beside the verdict.", "The deltaPLL of the firewall after discounting, which the engine substitutes for 2e-3 whenever a benefit rate is given."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dividing the benefit''s present value by the VPF counts fatalities at a discount, and the course derives 0.034337 only to show the wrong route: 321062.02 over it gives 9350246.71. The engine divides by deltaPLL times the life, 0.040000, for 8026550.41. It is no ratio of cost to benefit, and deltaPLL stays 2e-3 whatever the rates.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'How does a caller type a benefit discount rate of 1.5 percent, and what range does the engine accept for a rate?', '["As 1.5, the engine dividing every rate by one hundred before it discounts.", "As 0.015, and any rate from 0 to 0.06, the span of the two HSE conventions.", "As 0.015, and any rate above -1 per year.", "As 0.015, and only the four rates the two HSE conventions name."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course''s units table says rates of discount and growth are per year, above -1, written 0.035 for 3.5 percent, so 1.5 percent is 0.015. The engine reads a rate as typed, it bounds rates only from below, and it takes any rate as an input rather than a list of conventions.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'Under R2P2''s convention the firewall''s benefit is uprated by 4 percent a year and discounted at 6 percent. Why is its present value 32946.93, below the undiscounted 40000.00?', '["Because the engine applies the growth rate only to the first year and discounts the other nineteen years at the full 0.06.", "Because growth and discount are both applied to the cost as well, and the benefit is then reduced in full to keep the ratio unchanged.", "Because R2P2 counts only the fatality benefit and drops the growth on injuries, where the 4 percent applies.", "Because the discount at 0.06 outpaces the growth at 0.04, so each later year''s uprated benefit is still worth less today."]'::jsonb, 3, 'With benefits growing at 0.04 and discounted at 0.06, each year-end flow is worth less than the year before in present terms, so the total 32946.93 falls below 40000.00, though above what a 0.06 discount alone would give. Growth applies to every year''s benefit, costs carry no growth, and the firewall has no injury lines.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'Of the three conventions, which gives the EDIKAN firewall its lowest ICAF, and why?', '["The undiscounted convention, 8750000.00, because a cost that is not discounted is always the smallest of the three present values of cost.", "R2P2 Appendix 3''s, 7683740.15, because its 6 percent on costs discounts the cost most, while the fatalities prevented stay at 0.040000.", "The 2003 checklist limits, 8026550.41, because its benefit rate of 1.5 percent is the lowest and the ICAF follows the benefit.", "All three give the same ICAF, since the ICAF counts fatalities undiscounted and so no convention can move it at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ICAF is the present value of the cost over 0.040000 fatalities prevented, so the convention that discounts the cost most gives the lowest: R2P2''s 6 percent brings the cost to 307349.61 and the ICAF to 7683740.15. Undiscounted, the cost is largest, the ICAF does not involve the benefit rate, and the cost side does move.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'The row labelled ''the 2003 checklist limits'' uses 0.015 on benefits and 0.035 on costs. Why are they called limits?', '["Because the checklist bounds the rates, benefits discounted at no more than 1.5 percent and costs at no less than 3.5 percent, and the row takes each bound.", "Because the checklist fixes the rates exactly at those values for every duty holder, and any other pair is refused by the engine as outside its range.", "Because the rates are the largest the engine will accept for a benefit and the smallest for a cost, which are hard limits built into the call.", "Because the checklist prints them as the limits of a sensitivity range, and the engine runs the test at both ends of each range automatically."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course says the CBA checklist discounts benefits at no more than 1.5 percent and costs at no less than 3.5 percent, and the row sits at those bounds. The engine takes any rate above -1 as an input, so nothing is refused or hard coded, and it runs one call per convention, never a range.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'Stepping the factor to 5 lifts the most the firewall may cost to 200000.00. Does it pass there?', '["NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because 200000.00 is more than the benefit of 40000.00.", "NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because the ratio of 8.750000 is within one DF of 10.", "GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because the cost of 350000.00 is greater than 200000.00.", "NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because 200000.00 is below the ICAF."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The largest reasonably practicable cost is DF times the benefit, and a cost above it fails: 350000.00 is greater than 200000.00, so the DF sweep prints GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE at 5. The limit is compared with the cost, never with the benefit. The ratio is compared with the DF of 5 in hand, and the ICAF is a cost per fatality, which is no limit on the cost.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'The engine''s verdict can be read two equivalent ways. Which statement says the same thing as cost / benefit > DF?', '["The ICAF of the measure exceeds the VPF, which is DF times the benefit per fatality once every injury line has been added into the VPF.", "The benefit exceeds DF times the cost, which is the largest reasonable benefit the engine reports beside the verdict.", "The cost exceeds the benefit plus the DF, which is the margin the checklist allows above the benefit of a measure.", "The present value of the cost exceeds the largest reasonably practicable cost, which is DF times the present value of the benefit."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Multiplying both sides of cost / benefit > DF by the benefit gives cost > DF x benefit, and DF x benefit is the largest reasonably practicable cost the engine returns. The ICAF leaves injuries out and the VPF includes none, putting the benefit over DF times the cost reverses the inequality, and the DF multiplies the benefit rather than adding to it.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'What is the smallest DF at which the undiscounted EDIKAN firewall would be NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE?', '["A DF equal to its ratio, 8.750000, because a cost exactly DF times the benefit belongs to the lower band.", "Any DF above 8.750000, strictly, because a ratio equal to the DF is grossly disproportionate under the checklist''s test.", "A DF of 10, because that is the first factor in the DF sweep at which the verdict turns, and no DF between is permitted.", "A DF of 3, the firewall''s stated DF, the only one the engine will weigh."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ratio is 8.750000, and the test is strictly greater, so at a DF equal to the ratio the cost is exactly DF times the benefit and the verdict is NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE by the lower band rule. DF 10 is only the next swept factor; any DF of 1 or more is accepted, and the stated DF of 3 is one choice among many.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'At the 2003 checklist limits the firewall''s ratio is 9.350247. What verdict does the engine return at a DF of 10?', '["GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because discounting raises the ratio above its undiscounted 8.750000.", "NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because 9.350247 is below 10.", "GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because at the checklist limits the DF must be taken as 3 in every case.", "NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because the ICAF of 8026550.41 is below the checklist''s VPF of 1336800."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The verdict compares the ratio with the DF: 9.350247 is not greater than 10, so the measure is not grossly disproportionate. Discounting did raise the ratio, but not above 10. The DF is the caller''s input at any convention. The ICAF of 8026550.41 is above 1336800, and the firewall is weighed at a VPF of 1000000 anyway, so that reasoning fails twice.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'What does the cost to benefit ratio of 8.750000 say about the undiscounted EDIKAN firewall, in plain words?', '["That the firewall prevents 8.750000 fatalities over its life, which is the count the ICAF then divides the cost by.", "That the firewall must run 8.750000 years before its benefit repays its cost, the point in its life at which the measure breaks even.", "That its cost over the life is 8.750000 times the value of the fatalities it prevents over the life, both undiscounted.", "That the firewall''s cost exceeds the largest reasonably practicable cost at DF 3 by 8.750000 times over the life."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ratio is the present value of the cost, 350000.00, over the present value of the benefit, 40000.00, and with every rate at zero both are plain sums. The fatalities prevented are 0.040000. The ratio is no time to repay a cost, and the cost exceeds the DF 3 limit of 120000.00 by a smaller factor than 8.750000.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'The engine returns NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE for a measure. What does that verdict oblige the duty holder to do?', '["Nothing in particular, since the verdict only says the measure is not unreasonable and adoption stays at the duty holder''s discretion.", "Adopt it only if the individual risk is UNACCEPTABLE, since a measure is required only where the individual risk exceeds the upper limit.", "Weigh it again at a DF of 10, since a measure is adopted only if it also passes at the highest DF the checklist regards as likely.", "Adopt the measure, because a measure whose cost is not grossly disproportionate to its benefit is reasonably practicable."]'::jsonb, 3, 'NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE means the measure is reasonably practicable, and the course says the duty to adopt it stands, even at the boundary. ALARP is a duty in the TOLERABLE region, where reducing an individual risk further is required when practicable, and the DF is a stated choice, which is no second hurdle.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'What does a DF of exactly 1 accept, and how does the undiscounted firewall fare at it?', '["A DF of 1 is allowed, since DFs vary from upwards of 1; at it the firewall is GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, its cost far above the benefit of 40000.00.", "A DF of 1 is refused, since a DF must lie strictly above 1; the firewall cannot be weighed at it and the call returns the engine''s refusal.", "A DF of 1 is allowed, and at it the firewall is NOT_GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, because the cost equals the benefit at a DF of exactly 1.", "A DF of 1 is treated as the checklist''s default of 10, so the firewall passes, with a largest reasonable cost of 400000.00 at that DF."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine refuses only a DF below 1, and the sweep prints DF 1 with a largest reasonable cost of 40000.00 and the verdict GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE, since the cost is 350000.00. A DF of 1 is inside the range, the cost is nowhere near the benefit, and no DF is ever replaced by another.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'Purple Book Appendix 6.B''s last step is run through `locationIndividualRisk` with f PM Pphi = 1.84e-8 per year and the printed Pd of 0.381. What comes back, and what does the source print?', '["0.000000006997 per year, which the source prints as 7e-9 and which follows from the printed Pd alone.", "0.000000007010 per year, which the source prints as 7e-9.", "0.000000007010 per year, which the source prints as 7.0104e-9 exactly, matching the engine to five figures.", "0.380294556093 per year, which is the whole chain''s Pd reported as though it were the contribution."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Through `locationIndividualRisk`, 1.84e-8 times 0.381 is 0.000000007010, and the source prints 7e-9. 0.000000006997 is the whole chain''s contribution, which rests on the golden whole-chain Pd of 0.380294556093. 7.0104e-9 is the step by step value from the printed figures, and the source prints only 7e-9. The last figure is a probability of death, which has no unit per year at all.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'Besides the rounded Pd, what else in Purple Book Appendix 6.B does the course flag as rounded or inconsistent in the source?', '["It uses 12 wind sectors where its own figure draws fewer, so the coverage probability it prints is overstated for the release.", "It uses a loss of containment frequency of 5e-7 per year where its own table prints a larger one, a slip of an order of magnitude.", "It uses R = 361 m for a point at (200, 300), whose distance computes to 360.555 m, and one step names the point (100, 200).", "It prints the weather and direction probability 0.0368 where the engine computes a different figure from the same inputs."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course says the example uses R = 361 m for a point whose distance computes to 360.555 m from its coordinates (200, 300), and one step names the point (100, 200); a reproduction that needs a rounded intermediate says which one. The 12 sectors and the frequency of 5e-7 are the golden inputs, and 0.0368 is reproduced exactly.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'Purple Book Table 4.5 prints its middle band as "10 - 100 kg/s". How does the engine read the edges, and what status does its validation record give them?', '["Open at both ends, so exactly 10 and 100 kg/s fall in the outer bands; the record labels every cell of the table published, the band edges included.", "Closed at 10 and open at 100 kg/s, as a half-open range; the record labels the band edges published and the interior cells the engine''s reading.", "Open at 10 and closed at 100 kg/s, following the lower band convention; the record labels the band edges a decision the owner took.", "Closed at both ends, so exactly 10 and 100 kg/s fall in the middle band; the record labels the band edge cells the engine''s own reading and the interior cells published."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The basis says "table lookup; the middle band is closed at both ends", and the course''s table puts 10 and 100 kg/s in the medium band. The validation record labels the band edge cells as the engine''s own reading and the interior cells as published. The lower band convention is about thresholds of individual risk and cost, which is a separate matter.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'Why does every graded exercise in this course state its ignition probabilities and its vapour cloud split instead of asking for a lookup?', '["Because the Table 4.5 cells and the 0.6 and 0.4 split are transcriptions of one source, read once, with nothing in the validation checking them against a second reading.", "Because the lookup refuses any rate or mass that is not exactly one of the five in the table, so a graded exercise has to state the value.", "Because the table and the split belong to the consequence course, which grades them in its own capstones and forbids this course from using them.", "Because the split changes with the substance class, and a graded exercise cannot state a substance class without giving the answer away."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course says the table cells and the split are single transcriptions and nothing in the validation checks them against a second reading, so every capstone states the values it uses. The lookup takes any positive rate or mass and returns its band. The table is taught in this course, and the preset split is one pair for every substance.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'The engine has three functions that call the consequence engine to MAKE a probability of death. What does this course do with them?', '["It teaches their inputs at Expert, since an analyst who weighs a measure must first compute the probability of death that the measure changes.", "It never asks for their inputs: `toxicPlumeGridPointRisk`, `thermalFatalityTransect` and `poolFireFatalityTransect` sit on the consequence course''s side of the seam.", "It uses them to grade the probability of death in every capstone, since a stated Pd would let the learner skip the consequence arithmetic.", "It replaces them with the Purple Book Table 5.3 fractions, which give a probability of death at any place without any consequence model."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course says it does not teach their inputs and grades nothing through them; a probability of death is a stated input that belongs to the consequence course. The Table 5.3 fractions are the share of a population indoors for societal risk, and they produce no probability of death at a place.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'Along the EREMOR transect the LSIR crosses 1e-5 per year at 126.982958 m. Why is a crossing distance like this never graded?', '["Because the crossing depends on the wind rose, which the engine does not model, so the analyst must confirm the distance on a map of the plot plan.", "Because the engine rounds each crossing to the nearest point of the transect before it reports it, so the printed distance is approximate.", "Because the interpolation in log10 of the LSIR between bracketing points is a presentation rule the engine chose, and nothing published fixes it.", "Because a contour of 1e-5 per year is not one of the levels the Purple Book asks to be shown, so its crossing carries no weight at all."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course says a crossing is interpolated in log10 of the LSIR between the two bracketing points, linearly where one side is zero, and calls that a presentation rule the engine chose, which nothing published fixes. The transect is stated per scenario, so no wind rose enters, nothing is rounded to the grid, and 1e-5 is one of the five Purple Book contour levels.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'The Purple Book''s introduction to societal risk says "more than N" while its equation 6.6 says "N or more". Which reading does the engine follow, and what does the other one do to F(12) for the JISIKE off-site curve?', '["The introduction''s \"more than N\", giving F(12) = 0.000001700000; \"N or more\" would double count the corner and give 0.000009700000.", "The equation''s \"N or more\", giving F(12) = 0.000008000000; \"more than N\" would keep only the scenarios above twelve and give 0.000001700000.", "Neither, since the source contradicts itself; the engine reports both curves and leaves the choice of reading to the analyst in the note.", "The equation''s \"N or more\", giving F(12) = 0.000009700000; \"more than N\" would drop the corner''s own scenario and give 0.000001700000."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine follows the equation, and the course shows F(12) = 0.000009700000 for N or more and 0.000001700000 for more than N, which drops the flash fire at the road sitting exactly at N = 12. 0.000008000000 is the scenario at exactly 12 alone. "N or more" counts each scenario once, and the engine returns one curve.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'When does the ALARP note this course grades at Expert carry a societal picture beside the individual risk of the most exposed person?', '["If the population warrants it: the note carries the societal picture when there is a population whose deaths at once the F-N curve and PLL describe.", "Always, since every ALARP note must compare an F-N curve with the Dutch line, whatever the size of the population exposed to the facility and its hazards.", "Never, since the Expert ALARP note weighs measures only, and the societal picture belongs entirely to the Professional tier of the course.", "Only when the individual risk is UNACCEPTABLE, since a societal picture is needed only to argue that the facility should be shut."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The course''s note lists the individual risk of the most exposed person and its region, then the societal picture if the population warrants it. It is no fixed requirement, and nothing makes it depend on the individual risk band. Expert may use the Professional tier''s figures; the Dutch line is a historical published comparison the analyst chooses to make.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'The undiscounted EDIKAN ICAF is 8750000.00 and the VPF is 1000000. Read beside each other in the note at the stated DF of 3, what do they say?', '["The ICAF is below ten times the VPF, so the firewall passes at the stated DF of 3, and the verdict of GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE must be an error in the call.", "The ICAF is more than three times the VPF, so with no injury lines the firewall fails at DF 3, which agrees with the verdict GROSSLY_DISPROPORTIONATE.", "The ICAF and the VPF cannot be compared, since the ICAF is a cost per fatality and the VPF is a value per fatality in a different currency.", "The ICAF is 8.750000 times the VPF, which means the firewall prevents 8.750000 fatalities, and the note reports that count of deaths beside the verdict."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The course says a measure whose ICAF is below VPF times the DF passes when injuries add nothing. 8750000.00 is well above three times 1000000, so the firewall fails at DF 3, in agreement with the verdict. Below ten times the VPF is the DF 10 reading. Both are money per fatality in the currency of the inputs, and the firewall prevents 0.040000 fatalities.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'A note bands the operator''s individual risk and then weighs a measure by its deltaPLL. How are the two quantities related?', '["They are the same quantity, since the operator''s IRPA and the PLL are both per year and a measure lowers each of them by the same amount.", "The deltaPLL is the fall in the operator''s IRPA, since the benefit of a measure is valued only for the most exposed person on the roster.", "The band is one person''s IRPA against the limits; deltaPLL is the fall in expected fatalities per year across everyone exposed, the input to the benefit.", "The band is of the PLL, and the deltaPLL is the fall in the band, measured in the number of regions the measure moves the facility down."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The course separates them: IRPA is the individual risk one person carries, banded against the R2P2 limits, and PLL is expected fatalities per year across a population, whose reduction deltaPLL times the VPF is the benefit. They are different quantities, a measure''s benefit counts every fatality prevented, and a band is never of the PLL.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'An off-site resident''s individual risk is to be banded in the note. Which preset applies, and what changes against the worker preset?', '["`r2p2-workers`, since the resident''s individual risk comes from the same facility and one preset serves everyone it affects.", "`r2p2-public`, whose lower limit is 1e-7 per year against the workers'' 1e-6, with the same upper limit of 1e-3 per year.", "Neither preset, since R2P2 has no limits for people off the site and the analyst must give thresholds of their own.", "`r2p2-public`, whose upper limit is 1e-4 per year against the workers'' 1e-3, with the same lower limit of 1e-6."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A member of the public is banded against `r2p2-public`: unacceptable above 1e-4 per year and broadly acceptable at or below 1e-6, where workers are unacceptable above 1e-3 with the same lower limit. The preset follows who carries the individual risk, the lower limits match, and R2P2 does give public limits.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'Why does the note quote the ratio the engine returns beside the band, such as the supervisor''s 8.743500 times the lower limit?', '["Because a band word alone says only which region; the ratios say how far into it the individual risk sits, which the demonstration has to address.", "Because the ratio is the graded figure and the band word is decoration, which the note may leave out once the ratio is quoted.", "Because the ratio converts the individual risk into a probability of death for the individual, which R2P2 compares with its limits instead of the band.", "Because the ratio is the factor by which a measure must reduce the individual risk, which the engine and the note then treat as the DF of that measure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine returns the ratios to both thresholds beside the band, and the course quotes the supervisor at 8.743500 times the lower limit to show where in the TOLERABLE region the individual risk lies. The band still matters to the note. The ratio is no probability of death, and the DF is a stated input for the cost-benefit test with no link to this ratio.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'An analyst writing up a worker who sits on the upper limit wants to argue for the stricter reading of that limit. What may the ALARP note do?', '["Rerun the engine with its option that puts threshold values in the upper band, which the engine offers to analysts for exactly this purpose.", "Report the engine''s result as it stands and argue in words, from R2P2''s bias to safety, that the stricter reading is defensible, since the note carries judgement the band does not.", "Report the band as UNACCEPTABLE in place of the engine''s word, since an analyst may overwrite any band word they disagree with in the note.", "Nothing, since the lower band convention binds every note and no argument against a convention of the engine may be written into one."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The lower band rule is a declared choice, and the course names R2P2''s bias to safety as the argument the other way; the note is where the analyst''s judgement is written, beside the result the engine returned. The engine has no option to flip the convention, and a note that altered the engine''s band word would misreport the calculation.'),
  ('qra', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'The EDIKAN firewall is weighed with every rate left at its default. Why must the note still state the rates?', '["It need not, since a rate left at its default has no effect on the result of any call, which is why the engine defaults it in the first place.", "Because the engine refuses a note that leaves out the rates, and the ALARP note is passed back into the engine for a final check of its figures.", "Because a rate of zero is itself a convention, undiscounted, and the verdict can turn on the discounting convention the analyst chooses.", "Because the checklist forbids undiscounted weighing, so the note must explain why its rates were left at zero against that rule."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Every rate defaults to zero, which is undiscounted, and the course says the verdict can turn on the discounting convention, so a demonstration states each choice. A rate of zero changes the result as surely as any other rate. The note is a document, never an engine input, and the checklist''s own example is undiscounted.');
